WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:06.700
Hi, everyone.

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:14.180
So today's the last lecture
of lossless compression.

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:19.250
And so we'll look at one
of the most important

00:00:19.250 --> 00:00:21.860
lossless compression
technique, LZ77.

00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:25.100
And in the second half
today, we will look at some--

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:27.410
sort of to conclude the
lossless compression

00:00:27.410 --> 00:00:29.240
part, we will look at
some practical tips

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:32.630
on lossless compression, based
on mine and other people's

00:00:32.630 --> 00:00:34.940
experience working
with compression.

00:00:38.470 --> 00:00:40.060
So in the last
couple of lectures,

00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:43.450
we have been looking at
compression for non-iid data,

00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:48.310
and we started looking at
conditional entropy, right,

00:00:48.310 --> 00:00:49.690
conditional probabilities.

00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:51.760
And then we went into
probability rate,

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:53.775
which was, for the
purpose of this lecture,

00:00:53.775 --> 00:00:55.900
all you need to know is,
it's the fundamental limit

00:00:55.900 --> 00:00:58.900
of lossless compression
for stationary sources.

00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:01.480
Just like entropy is the
fundamental limit for iid

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:04.090
sources and conditional entropy
is the fundamental limit

00:01:04.090 --> 00:01:06.100
for Markov sources, right?

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:09.640
And if you recall, we defined
the entropy rate in two ways,

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:12.940
one is, like, the incremental
conditional probability,

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:14.920
what is the limit as
n goes to infinity,

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:16.720
and the other is
that if you think

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:18.790
in terms of blocks
of size n, what

00:01:18.790 --> 00:01:22.840
is the average entropy per block
as n becomes larger and larger.

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:24.940
And then in the last
lecture, we looked

00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:26.980
at context-based
arithmetic coding

00:01:26.980 --> 00:01:29.590
where we will end this
very important concept,

00:01:29.590 --> 00:01:31.090
that prediction
implies compression.

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:31.590
Right?

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:34.060
If you have a good predictor,
that's a good compressor.

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:37.370
And then, we looked at some
specific models for prediction,

00:01:37.370 --> 00:01:39.500
k-th order adaptive
arithmetic coding,

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:42.620
which we had a quiz
question on, and we

00:01:42.620 --> 00:01:49.230
looked at some more advanced
models in less detail.

00:01:49.230 --> 00:01:51.690
But then, we looked at the
most advanced model of all

00:01:51.690 --> 00:01:54.480
in more detail, which was
the large language models,

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:57.615
and we looked at how you can
push compression to its limits.

00:02:01.050 --> 00:02:03.720
All of this was based on very
carefully modeling the data

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:06.375
or learning the probability
distribution of the data.

00:02:06.375 --> 00:02:08.250
Today, we will see a
very different technique

00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:10.680
where you don't do that,
basically, and still

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:11.775
achieve excellent results.

00:02:14.450 --> 00:02:16.340
OK, so lots to cover today.

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:19.640
So what I'm going to do is, I
will not spend a lot of time

00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:21.230
on the quiz.

00:02:21.230 --> 00:02:23.840
So for the first question,
you had a sequence

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:27.050
that you wanted to encode
with the first order adaptive

00:02:27.050 --> 00:02:28.370
arithmetic coding, right.

00:02:28.370 --> 00:02:31.910
We did an example of this
in class, very similar.

00:02:31.910 --> 00:02:35.750
And you will have some practice
in your homework as well.

00:02:35.750 --> 00:02:38.660
But what I did was, I
created this table for you,

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:40.670
just to make sure
you understand, like,

00:02:40.670 --> 00:02:41.720
how to do this right.

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:44.090
So, at every step, you
have the previous symbol,

00:02:44.090 --> 00:02:47.420
you have the current symbol,
you have the counts before,

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:52.420
and then you update the counts
so you get the counts after.

00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:55.270
And please, take
a look at these.

00:02:55.270 --> 00:02:57.550
Unless anybody has any
specific question on the quiz,

00:02:57.550 --> 00:02:59.860
I will skip this
question for now.

00:03:03.830 --> 00:03:05.540
OK, yeah.

00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:07.850
Please talk to us
after class if you

00:03:07.850 --> 00:03:09.950
didn't get any of
these or these answers

00:03:09.950 --> 00:03:13.418
are not what you
thought they were.

00:03:13.418 --> 00:03:15.710
The second question, or the
first question in the quiz,

00:03:15.710 --> 00:03:19.190
maybe let's talk
about that a bit more.

00:03:19.190 --> 00:03:22.370
So the first question was, you
know that your data is roughly

00:03:22.370 --> 00:03:25.387
second order Markov but do
not know the actual transition

00:03:25.387 --> 00:03:25.970
probabilities.

00:03:25.970 --> 00:03:27.710
And we asked you
whether you should

00:03:27.710 --> 00:03:30.770
use context-based arithmetic
coding or adaptive arithmetic

00:03:30.770 --> 00:03:31.460
coding.

00:03:31.460 --> 00:03:33.695
How many think
it's the first one?

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:38.560
Second one?

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:39.190
OK.

00:03:39.190 --> 00:03:40.300
So, correct, right.

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:42.947
Adaptive just means that you
don't assume some initial model

00:03:42.947 --> 00:03:44.530
that you know, and
you learn the model

00:03:44.530 --> 00:03:47.380
from the data which just seems
more appropriate for-- when

00:03:47.380 --> 00:03:49.720
you don't know the
sequence-- the probabilities.

00:03:53.230 --> 00:03:54.900
Here, it's a third
order Markov and you

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:56.170
know the exact distribution.

00:03:56.170 --> 00:03:57.490
So since you know
the distribution,

00:03:57.490 --> 00:03:59.740
there is no point in trying
to learn it from the data.

00:03:59.740 --> 00:04:01.600
That's just less efficient.

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:03.400
And therefore,
here, the answer is,

00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:07.107
you just do the context-based
arithmetic coding.

00:04:07.107 --> 00:04:08.940
There was a third
question in the quiz which

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:10.620
we had to remove
because I didn't

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:12.960
cover the material in time.

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:15.880
You know nothing
about the input data.

00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:18.160
So you have literally
no idea what's in there.

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:20.320
What would you do in that case?

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:20.950
Right?

00:04:20.950 --> 00:04:23.710
And actually, this is
what you will do, LZ77.

00:04:23.710 --> 00:04:25.387
That is the topic
of today's lecture,

00:04:25.387 --> 00:04:27.595
where you don't make any
assumptions about your data.

00:04:30.450 --> 00:04:32.850
OK, so before I
start, any questions

00:04:32.850 --> 00:04:34.125
on the previous material?

00:04:38.630 --> 00:04:39.130
OK.

00:04:47.050 --> 00:04:51.280
--did his-- 1948 paper
people worked on iid sources.

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:54.030
They did this
context-based stuff.

00:04:54.030 --> 00:04:57.020
But nobody had an
idea, whether for

00:04:57.020 --> 00:04:58.770
a general stationary
distribution, can you

00:04:58.770 --> 00:05:01.590
have a compressor that works for
any distribution, irrespective

00:05:01.590 --> 00:05:04.230
of, like, don't need to know
the distribution in advance.

00:05:04.230 --> 00:05:07.290
And then, there were
A sequence of papers

00:05:07.290 --> 00:05:12.690
by Abraham Lempel and
Jacob Ziv at Bell Labs

00:05:12.690 --> 00:05:16.890
at the time, who basically just
change the world of compression

00:05:16.890 --> 00:05:20.760
in 1977 and '78.

00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:23.760
So what we'll study today
is something-- the concept

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:25.530
of universal compressors.

00:05:25.530 --> 00:05:27.240
A universal
compressor is a scheme

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:29.790
that does well on
any stationary input

00:05:29.790 --> 00:05:32.730
without prior knowledge of
the source distribution.

00:05:32.730 --> 00:05:37.800
So it assumes nothing and still
gets you the optimal results.

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:41.370
And as part of that, we'll
look at really the most common

00:05:41.370 --> 00:05:45.270
scheme used in compression
today, the zip compressor,

00:05:45.270 --> 00:05:46.650
as you might have heard of.

00:05:49.730 --> 00:05:53.180
So let me define
universal compression,

00:05:53.180 --> 00:05:55.460
just to be slightly formal.

00:05:55.460 --> 00:05:56.990
So you have a compressor.

00:05:56.990 --> 00:05:59.130
The compressor has
some length function,

00:05:59.130 --> 00:06:02.420
which is the number of bits
it takes to describe xn.

00:06:02.420 --> 00:06:09.310
And it is universal if the
expected length per symbol

00:06:09.310 --> 00:06:13.840
matches the entropy rate for
any stationary ergodic source.

00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:15.940
Ergodic it is just a
technical condition,

00:06:15.940 --> 00:06:18.170
no need to worry about it.

00:06:18.170 --> 00:06:19.900
But really, for any
stationary source.

00:06:19.900 --> 00:06:23.170
So for any stationary source,
it achieves the entropy rate.

00:06:23.170 --> 00:06:27.202
No qualifiers, no
mention that it

00:06:27.202 --> 00:06:28.910
will achieve the
entropy for a k-th order

00:06:28.910 --> 00:06:30.650
but not for a k plus
1 i-th order, no.

00:06:30.650 --> 00:06:32.540
For any stationary
source, however

00:06:32.540 --> 00:06:34.560
complicated it might be.

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:38.810
So really a single compressor
is asymptotically optimal.

00:06:38.810 --> 00:06:41.240
Asymptotically because
this happens in the limit

00:06:41.240 --> 00:06:42.920
for every distribution.

00:06:45.520 --> 00:06:48.610
For a long time before Lempel
and Ziv did their work people,

00:06:48.610 --> 00:06:49.860
believed this was impossible.

00:06:49.860 --> 00:06:52.420
So even at the time
and even today,

00:06:52.420 --> 00:06:56.560
this is a very surprising result
that such a thing is possible.

00:06:56.560 --> 00:06:57.520
And we'll see.

00:06:57.520 --> 00:06:58.900
It's actually very simple.

00:07:04.860 --> 00:07:06.570
Another sort of on
the flip side a way

00:07:06.570 --> 00:07:10.530
to think about this in terms
of universal prediction,

00:07:10.530 --> 00:07:13.140
recall from last lecture
that every compressor

00:07:13.140 --> 00:07:14.790
induces a distribution.

00:07:14.790 --> 00:07:17.370
So for any compressor
which gives you

00:07:17.370 --> 00:07:20.670
a length function, l,
you get a p-hat, which

00:07:20.670 --> 00:07:23.380
is 2 power minus l.

00:07:23.380 --> 00:07:28.510
And what this is saying is that
a universal compressor's p-hat,

00:07:28.510 --> 00:07:32.980
the distribution is learning
over the data approximates

00:07:32.980 --> 00:07:36.940
any stationary distribution
arbitrarily closely as n grows.

00:07:36.940 --> 00:07:42.080
So just as like a
rough figure, so this

00:07:42.080 --> 00:07:50.110
is like the p for different
stationary distributions,

00:07:50.110 --> 00:07:52.330
the distribution for
all of them, basically.

00:07:55.720 --> 00:08:01.250
And somehow, there is this
one point here, p-hat LZ,

00:08:01.250 --> 00:08:03.740
which is close to every other
point in the distribution.

00:08:03.740 --> 00:08:05.540
So there exists one
single distribution

00:08:05.540 --> 00:08:09.170
that's a good approximator
of every other distribution.

00:08:12.520 --> 00:08:17.320
So I spoke of it in
a very handwavy way,

00:08:17.320 --> 00:08:20.320
you need to rigorously formulate
it, which we won't do here.

00:08:20.320 --> 00:08:24.430
But talk to Tsachy, he's like
an expert in these matters.

00:08:24.430 --> 00:08:27.070
He has extended the theory
of universal compression

00:08:27.070 --> 00:08:30.610
to universal denoising,
universal lossy compression.

00:08:30.610 --> 00:08:34.240
And look at his lecture notes,
or whenever he teaches it

00:08:34.240 --> 00:08:38.049
next time, this EE 376C,
which is completely focused

00:08:38.049 --> 00:08:40.150
on the theory of
universal compression

00:08:40.150 --> 00:08:43.030
and universal
denoising and so on.

00:08:43.030 --> 00:08:46.610
You can also look at this
paper if you are interested.

00:08:46.610 --> 00:08:49.492
But for now, let's
come back to LZ.

00:08:52.590 --> 00:08:54.300
Any questions on
the general concept

00:08:54.300 --> 00:08:57.480
of universal compression,
or universal prediction?

00:08:57.480 --> 00:08:59.573
We are just saying that
you have a compressor

00:08:59.573 --> 00:09:00.990
or you have a
predictor that works

00:09:00.990 --> 00:09:02.940
well for any
stationary distribution

00:09:02.940 --> 00:09:04.710
without making any assumptions.

00:09:04.710 --> 00:09:06.510
And we'll see how that happens.

00:09:11.100 --> 00:09:11.700
OK.

00:09:11.700 --> 00:09:17.910
So the LZ77, or Lempel-Ziv
family of universal algorithms,

00:09:17.910 --> 00:09:20.800
there are many, many
implementations.

00:09:20.800 --> 00:09:24.400
So they had two papers--
one in 1977, one in 1978,

00:09:24.400 --> 00:09:26.650
both of them describing
different schemes.

00:09:26.650 --> 00:09:31.180
The one in 1977 is called
LZ77, the other one LZ78,

00:09:31.180 --> 00:09:33.670
and then people made
modifications of those

00:09:33.670 --> 00:09:36.190
by adding different things.

00:09:36.190 --> 00:09:41.150
All of these are used in a bunch
of these different compressors.

00:09:41.150 --> 00:09:42.980
You see gzip and
zstandard, you see

00:09:42.980 --> 00:09:45.530
PNG, which is an
image compressor, ZIP,

00:09:45.530 --> 00:09:47.840
which is, again, a
very common compressor.

00:09:47.840 --> 00:09:52.340
You see GIF here,
which is image, right?

00:09:52.340 --> 00:09:56.465
And here are some references
if you want to look at these.

00:09:56.465 --> 00:09:58.340
We we'll look at some
of these in more detail

00:09:58.340 --> 00:10:01.292
as we move along the lecture.

00:10:01.292 --> 00:10:03.750
But this is just to say that
most of the common compressors

00:10:03.750 --> 00:10:06.953
today are built upon some
version of Lempel-Ziv.

00:10:10.060 --> 00:10:13.150
So I don't know if
you'll listen to music,

00:10:13.150 --> 00:10:16.080
or to this particular song.

00:10:16.080 --> 00:10:18.405
The idea of LZ77
algorithms is very simple.

00:10:21.500 --> 00:10:23.390
History repeats itself.

00:10:23.390 --> 00:10:25.520
You have some data,
you see some sequence,

00:10:25.520 --> 00:10:28.660
you have probably
seen that before

00:10:28.660 --> 00:10:30.670
in some form or the other.

00:10:30.670 --> 00:10:34.220
And if you have seen
it before, why not

00:10:34.220 --> 00:10:40.130
just put a pointer that I
saw this word 100 words ago.

00:10:40.130 --> 00:10:42.890
Or I saw this sentence,
1,000 sentences ago.

00:10:42.890 --> 00:10:44.330
Something like that.

00:10:44.330 --> 00:10:45.020
Right?

00:10:45.020 --> 00:10:47.540
This song in particular,
as you might imagine,

00:10:47.540 --> 00:10:50.270
is very well compressible
with this technique.

00:10:50.270 --> 00:10:51.500
It just repeats.

00:10:51.500 --> 00:10:53.945
Each line is a repeat
of the previous line.

00:10:58.520 --> 00:11:03.080
And [INAUDIBLE] last time made
the class listen to the song,

00:11:03.080 --> 00:11:06.050
so that was good.

00:11:06.050 --> 00:11:06.680
Right.

00:11:06.680 --> 00:11:07.940
So this is the idea, right.

00:11:07.940 --> 00:11:08.870
Idea is simple.

00:11:08.870 --> 00:11:12.050
Replace repeated segments in
data with pointers and lengths.

00:11:12.050 --> 00:11:15.080
So if we wanted to
draw it here, you

00:11:15.080 --> 00:11:20.290
would say that this
around the world--

00:11:20.290 --> 00:11:23.880
so this a is like
this whole thing is

00:11:23.880 --> 00:11:26.010
a match of this previous thing.

00:11:26.010 --> 00:11:28.880
So you need to
store some length.

00:11:28.880 --> 00:11:31.040
And they need to
store this offset.

00:11:31.040 --> 00:11:34.580
Offset is, like, how far in the
past has this thing occurred,

00:11:34.580 --> 00:11:38.100
and how long is the match.

00:11:38.100 --> 00:11:41.910
So with that in mind,
let's just do an example,

00:11:41.910 --> 00:11:44.400
I think will give you a
very good sense of what

00:11:44.400 --> 00:11:46.540
we are talking about.

00:11:46.540 --> 00:11:50.010
So what LZ77, that's the
version we will look at in more

00:11:50.010 --> 00:11:51.780
detail because that's
the one used in most

00:11:51.780 --> 00:11:53.490
of the popular algorithms.

00:11:56.350 --> 00:11:59.960
Starts-- you start with
a sequence like this.

00:11:59.960 --> 00:12:03.830
And then what you do is you
divide it into three streams,

00:12:03.830 --> 00:12:07.220
one is the unmatched
literals, the match length,

00:12:07.220 --> 00:12:11.690
and the match offset, and let's
understand these one by one.

00:12:11.690 --> 00:12:24.490
So OK.

00:12:24.490 --> 00:12:27.403
So, I see the first A here.

00:12:27.403 --> 00:12:28.570
This is the first character.

00:12:28.570 --> 00:12:29.980
I've never seen
it before clearly

00:12:29.980 --> 00:12:31.438
because that's the
first character.

00:12:31.438 --> 00:12:33.520
So that has to go into
an unmatched literal,

00:12:33.520 --> 00:12:35.830
there is no match.

00:12:35.830 --> 00:12:38.890
I see a B. I've never
seen a B before, it's

00:12:38.890 --> 00:12:43.620
the first B. So again, that
goes into unmatched literal.

00:12:43.620 --> 00:12:44.760
Now how about this guy?

00:12:44.760 --> 00:12:46.650
Have I seen a B before?

00:12:46.650 --> 00:12:48.240
Yeah, I just saw a B, right?

00:12:48.240 --> 00:12:50.220
So nice.

00:12:50.220 --> 00:12:52.490
I got a match.

00:12:52.490 --> 00:12:55.878
How long is the Match

00:12:55.878 --> 00:12:57.420
Right, the matches
just of length one

00:12:57.420 --> 00:12:58.795
because I can't
extend it, right,

00:12:58.795 --> 00:13:02.040
because I've seen B before
but I've never seen BA before.

00:13:02.040 --> 00:13:06.240
So it's a match of length
one at offset one, right,

00:13:06.240 --> 00:13:08.580
because it occurred just
one character before.

00:13:08.580 --> 00:13:10.390
OK.

00:13:10.390 --> 00:13:13.290
Now have I seen an A?

00:13:13.290 --> 00:13:16.330
Have seen A B?

00:13:16.330 --> 00:13:17.230
Have I seen ABB?

00:13:19.800 --> 00:13:22.950
Have I seen ABBA?

00:13:22.950 --> 00:13:25.590
Right, so here, I
see some disagreement

00:13:25.590 --> 00:13:27.720
among the students.

00:13:27.720 --> 00:13:32.400
I have indeed seen
an ABB, right, we

00:13:32.400 --> 00:13:34.470
are seeing this
ABBA here, and we'll

00:13:34.470 --> 00:13:37.920
come to this sort of overlapping
matches in a little bit.

00:13:37.920 --> 00:13:40.430
But for now, trust me.

00:13:40.430 --> 00:13:43.430
Have I seen ABBABB?

00:13:43.430 --> 00:13:46.391
Yes, I have.

00:13:46.391 --> 00:13:55.270
You see, this sequence is an
exact match of this sequence,

00:13:55.270 --> 00:13:56.260
right?

00:13:56.260 --> 00:13:59.260
At least, this I think we can
agree with that this ABBABB is

00:13:59.260 --> 00:14:00.700
match of the previous thing.

00:14:05.470 --> 00:14:09.613
So there was no unmatched
literal, nothing here.

00:14:09.613 --> 00:14:10.655
What is the match length?

00:14:19.000 --> 00:14:19.960
Six.

00:14:19.960 --> 00:14:21.870
I hope I heard a six.

00:14:21.870 --> 00:14:22.370
OK.

00:14:22.370 --> 00:14:23.120
What's the offset?

00:14:26.400 --> 00:14:29.700
How far is the start position
of-- how far is this from this,

00:14:29.700 --> 00:14:32.190
basically?

00:14:32.190 --> 00:14:33.090
It's a three, right?

00:14:33.090 --> 00:14:39.850
Because the previous are three
characters before, so this, OK.

00:14:39.850 --> 00:14:42.610
Now I get a C, C I've
never seen a match, right?

00:14:42.610 --> 00:14:44.350
So c is unmatched.

00:14:44.350 --> 00:14:49.152
And then I see AB, this one is
simple, I just saw an AB here.

00:14:49.152 --> 00:14:50.360
OK, this time please help me.

00:14:50.360 --> 00:14:51.410
What is the offset?

00:14:51.410 --> 00:14:52.400
The length is two.

00:14:52.400 --> 00:14:55.640
What is the offset, just to
make sure people are following?

00:14:59.400 --> 00:15:00.730
Can you repeat, please, sorry.

00:15:00.730 --> 00:15:01.230
Four.

00:15:01.230 --> 00:15:02.930
Four.

00:15:02.930 --> 00:15:05.900
Yep, so if you call this 0,
then this is minus 1, minus 2,

00:15:05.900 --> 00:15:08.115
minus 3, minus 4, yes, 4.

00:15:08.115 --> 00:15:08.615
Right.

00:15:12.630 --> 00:15:14.070
OK, that's it, basically.

00:15:14.070 --> 00:15:14.970
That's LZ77.

00:15:14.970 --> 00:15:18.480
That's how you pass a sequence
into these three streams.

00:15:18.480 --> 00:15:20.865
Unmatched literals, match
length, and match offset.

00:15:23.670 --> 00:15:27.570
For now, trust me for just
a second on this thing

00:15:27.570 --> 00:15:29.820
that this overlapping
match thing will be fine,

00:15:29.820 --> 00:15:33.060
and we'll do a
decoding very soon.

00:15:33.060 --> 00:15:34.860
Other than that, any questions?

00:15:39.830 --> 00:15:41.630
OK, good.

00:15:41.630 --> 00:15:43.850
You will get
experience on practice

00:15:43.850 --> 00:15:47.130
on this, as usual in the quiz.

00:15:47.130 --> 00:15:52.290
So, we did it at home,
we got the same things

00:15:52.290 --> 00:15:54.720
that we just got, right?

00:15:54.720 --> 00:15:57.060
Now we will decode it.

00:15:57.060 --> 00:15:59.280
OK, let's try to
decode the same thing.

00:15:59.280 --> 00:16:03.000
You know the answer, but let's
try to not cheat, and actually

00:16:03.000 --> 00:16:04.290
try to decode it.

00:16:04.290 --> 00:16:07.960
So, what do I start with?

00:16:07.960 --> 00:16:10.230
What is the first character?

00:16:14.680 --> 00:16:15.220
A, right?

00:16:15.220 --> 00:16:19.030
A and then followed by--

00:16:19.030 --> 00:16:25.040
A followed by a B, because you
first copy the unmatched part.

00:16:25.040 --> 00:16:28.130
Now you have a match length of
one and offset of one, right?

00:16:28.130 --> 00:16:29.840
So the character
that's coming here

00:16:29.840 --> 00:16:32.780
has to be, like, from
here, just one past,

00:16:32.780 --> 00:16:35.720
and it has a length, just
one length match, so ABB.

00:16:35.720 --> 00:16:36.830
OK.

00:16:36.830 --> 00:16:40.880
Now, you have a match length
of 6 and a match offset of 3.

00:16:40.880 --> 00:16:42.770
OK, so let's do it
character by character.

00:16:42.770 --> 00:16:44.330
Here, I have a offset of 3.

00:16:47.500 --> 00:16:49.270
So that goes here.

00:16:49.270 --> 00:16:58.330
I get A. Then I do the next
one, here, here, right?

00:16:58.330 --> 00:16:59.410
So far, I think--

00:16:59.410 --> 00:17:01.750
I hope we are all happy, right.

00:17:01.750 --> 00:17:03.910
Now we come to that
overlapping part.

00:17:03.910 --> 00:17:06.790
Now try to decode this thing.

00:17:06.790 --> 00:17:09.849
But the offset is 3 and this
guy is already decoded, right,

00:17:09.849 --> 00:17:10.960
this red part.

00:17:10.960 --> 00:17:13.900
So I can actually decode
this one, based on this one.

00:17:13.900 --> 00:17:18.339
This is again A,
this is a B. And this

00:17:18.339 --> 00:17:21.472
is a B. Was that clear?

00:17:21.472 --> 00:17:23.180
Basically, if you
sequentially decode it,

00:17:23.180 --> 00:17:26.599
it's fine if the matches
overlap with each other.

00:17:26.599 --> 00:17:29.550
Like the match overlaps
with itself, really.

00:17:29.550 --> 00:17:33.030
And then you get a C, which
is an unmatched literal.

00:17:33.030 --> 00:17:35.820
And then you have a
match length of two,

00:17:35.820 --> 00:17:39.940
so you have two things that
match at a offset of four.

00:17:39.940 --> 00:17:49.200
So, from here, you go here, so
A. And from here, you go here.

00:17:49.200 --> 00:17:51.920
That's B. OK.

00:17:51.920 --> 00:17:55.410
So just wanted to do
one example in class.

00:17:55.410 --> 00:17:59.580
So that you can do the
quiz question on your own.

00:17:59.580 --> 00:18:00.080
Right.

00:18:02.710 --> 00:18:04.870
OK.

00:18:04.870 --> 00:18:09.930
So, we have the decoding.

00:18:09.930 --> 00:18:13.065
So you can write this in the
form of a pseudocode, right?

00:18:13.065 --> 00:18:14.820
A very, very simple pseudocode.

00:18:14.820 --> 00:18:18.100
So for an input sequence , which
is like x 0, x 1 dot, dot, dot,

00:18:18.100 --> 00:18:18.600
right?

00:18:18.600 --> 00:18:22.860
And suppose we have
done until x i minus 1,

00:18:22.860 --> 00:18:28.870
then what you will try to do
is find the largest k, where

00:18:28.870 --> 00:18:30.595
k is the match length here.

00:18:36.300 --> 00:18:40.100
And i minus j is your offset.

00:18:40.100 --> 00:18:44.390
So this is basically telling
you that x i to x i plus k

00:18:44.390 --> 00:18:47.270
should match x j to x plus k.

00:18:47.270 --> 00:18:51.030
Just the definition of a
match, that it matches.

00:18:51.030 --> 00:18:56.270
And if no match found,
store as literal.

00:18:56.270 --> 00:18:57.710
That's it, basically.

00:18:57.710 --> 00:18:59.090
Very simple, basic idea.

00:19:02.270 --> 00:19:05.180
The decoding pseudocode,
you read any literals,

00:19:05.180 --> 00:19:07.730
and you copy to the output.

00:19:07.730 --> 00:19:11.680
And if you have a match
of length l and offset o,

00:19:11.680 --> 00:19:13.443
you basically copy
it over, right?

00:19:13.443 --> 00:19:14.860
And there are two
cases-- one case

00:19:14.860 --> 00:19:18.040
is if the length of the match
is less than the offset.

00:19:18.040 --> 00:19:21.820
So that's a scenario,
like, you are here,

00:19:21.820 --> 00:19:25.280
and this is your
length of the match,

00:19:25.280 --> 00:19:27.320
and this is your
offset, which is, like,

00:19:27.320 --> 00:19:29.060
much bigger than length.

00:19:29.060 --> 00:19:32.850
So then what you need
to do is you just

00:19:32.850 --> 00:19:36.990
copy this whole thing here it's
a very simple copy operation.

00:19:36.990 --> 00:19:37.770
Right?

00:19:37.770 --> 00:19:45.240
And the other case, so this
is like case I, l less than o,

00:19:45.240 --> 00:19:47.880
so length is less
than offset, and then

00:19:47.880 --> 00:19:54.020
the case II is where again
you have a match of length l

00:19:54.020 --> 00:19:56.360
but offset is very small.

00:19:56.360 --> 00:20:00.680
So in this case, what
happens is the thing

00:20:00.680 --> 00:20:03.910
overlaps as we just
saw, and then you

00:20:03.910 --> 00:20:07.510
have to be more careful
when you're decoding it.

00:20:07.510 --> 00:20:09.130
But what we did it just now.

00:20:09.130 --> 00:20:10.630
If you do it by
hand, you can do it.

00:20:10.630 --> 00:20:12.940
If you do it in code,
you can, again, do it.

00:20:12.940 --> 00:20:13.855
So OK.

00:20:17.390 --> 00:20:19.730
So this question
will be in your quiz.

00:20:23.110 --> 00:20:28.210
You will just do the unparsing,
basically of this guy,

00:20:28.210 --> 00:20:30.820
we'll ask you to decode it,
and I think if you decode it,

00:20:30.820 --> 00:20:33.550
then you've understood
the algorithm well enough.

00:20:33.550 --> 00:20:36.580
And actually we
encoded it in a way

00:20:36.580 --> 00:20:39.538
that doesn't match the way we--

00:20:39.538 --> 00:20:41.080
one thing you will
learn very quickly

00:20:41.080 --> 00:20:44.050
is that there are many ways
to encode it with LZ77.

00:20:44.050 --> 00:20:46.182
For example, maybe
you are very lazy,

00:20:46.182 --> 00:20:47.890
you don't try to match
anything, you just

00:20:47.890 --> 00:20:49.300
put everything as a literal.

00:20:49.300 --> 00:20:52.960
That's a valid LZ77 encoding.

00:20:52.960 --> 00:20:55.300
Or you try it very hard
and find the best matches.

00:20:55.300 --> 00:20:58.030
That's also valid LZ77 encoding.

00:20:58.030 --> 00:21:01.270
Maybe you don't allow your match
lengths to be more than 100,

00:21:01.270 --> 00:21:01.990
for some reason.

00:21:01.990 --> 00:21:03.040
That's a valid thing.

00:21:03.040 --> 00:21:04.780
So there are many
encoding choices

00:21:04.780 --> 00:21:07.870
you can make that all work with
the same decoding algorithm.

00:21:12.220 --> 00:21:15.850
OK any questions so far?

00:21:15.850 --> 00:21:17.810
Hopefully this is good.

00:21:17.810 --> 00:21:18.310
OK.

00:21:22.560 --> 00:21:25.970
So, encoding step.

00:21:25.970 --> 00:21:28.880
Start with the input data,
do this LZ77 parsing,

00:21:28.880 --> 00:21:32.370
you get your literals
and you get your matches.

00:21:32.370 --> 00:21:34.980
And then you do some
sort of entropy coding.

00:21:34.980 --> 00:21:37.815
You use one of your things
like Huffman coding or ANS

00:21:37.815 --> 00:21:40.560
or arithmetic coding, which
we have been listening about.

00:21:40.560 --> 00:21:43.910
And then you get
the compressed file.

00:21:43.910 --> 00:21:46.250
Different implementations
differ in this,

00:21:46.250 --> 00:21:51.380
and we'll come back to this
in much more detail shortly.

00:21:51.380 --> 00:21:55.130
But before we do that,
let me spend not too much

00:21:55.130 --> 00:21:58.460
time but a little time
talking about why this

00:21:58.460 --> 00:22:00.140
works, why is this optimal.

00:22:00.140 --> 00:22:03.470
We made a very strong
claim that LZ77

00:22:03.470 --> 00:22:05.120
works for any
stationary sequence,

00:22:05.120 --> 00:22:08.000
irrespective of
the distribution.

00:22:08.000 --> 00:22:11.270
So we should at least try to
get some intuition into that,

00:22:11.270 --> 00:22:12.605
if not the full proof.

00:22:15.880 --> 00:22:17.650
OK, so consider
this iid sequence.

00:22:17.650 --> 00:22:18.910
You just have an iid sequence.

00:22:22.120 --> 00:22:24.310
And if you have a
symbol, like value a,

00:22:24.310 --> 00:22:27.430
for example, which has
probability p of a,

00:22:27.430 --> 00:22:31.510
what is the expected gap between
consecutive occurrences of a?

00:22:31.510 --> 00:22:35.800
How often would consecutive
occurrences of a b on average?

00:22:38.820 --> 00:22:40.200
OK, tell me one thing.

00:22:40.200 --> 00:22:42.240
If a has a high
probability, would you

00:22:42.240 --> 00:22:44.310
occur the gap between
consecutive a's

00:22:44.310 --> 00:22:45.735
to be more or less?

00:22:50.130 --> 00:22:50.630
Less, right?

00:22:50.630 --> 00:22:53.213
Because if it a is more common,
it will occur very frequently.

00:22:53.213 --> 00:22:55.640
So the gap between consecutive
a's will be small, OK.

00:22:55.640 --> 00:22:59.780
And if a is less likely then
there will be a big gap between

00:22:59.780 --> 00:23:01.125
consecutive a's.

00:23:01.125 --> 00:23:01.625
OK.

00:23:04.540 --> 00:23:07.120
Let's try to do it in two steps.

00:23:07.120 --> 00:23:09.820
So in a block of size
n, how many times

00:23:09.820 --> 00:23:11.340
do you expect to see a?

00:23:17.140 --> 00:23:20.200
S iid, the probability
of a is P a.

00:23:29.548 --> 00:23:31.520
And multiply by P a.

00:23:31.520 --> 00:23:32.090
Yes.

00:23:32.090 --> 00:23:33.855
And multiplied by P a, right.

00:23:33.855 --> 00:23:34.355
Why?

00:23:37.650 --> 00:23:42.392
Each [INAUDIBLE]
probability of P a.

00:23:42.392 --> 00:23:48.370
[INAUDIBLE] expectation of the
sum of all those [INAUDIBLE]..

00:23:48.370 --> 00:23:51.580
Yeah, so the answer was, each of
them has a probability of p a,

00:23:51.580 --> 00:23:55.840
of being a, therefore the
expected number of occurrences

00:23:55.840 --> 00:23:57.940
will be n times P a.

00:23:57.940 --> 00:24:00.520
But as we as we saw
in the AP lecture,

00:24:00.520 --> 00:24:03.360
or you might have seen
before, you can say much more.

00:24:03.360 --> 00:24:05.110
You can actually say
that it will actually

00:24:05.110 --> 00:24:07.210
be very close to n,
n times P a because

00:24:07.210 --> 00:24:08.390
of the law of large numbers.

00:24:08.390 --> 00:24:08.890
Right?

00:24:08.890 --> 00:24:13.180
So that is correct
and as n gets larger,

00:24:13.180 --> 00:24:15.070
you actually see it
with a high probability,

00:24:15.070 --> 00:24:18.110
being close to n times P a.

00:24:18.110 --> 00:24:25.010
OK, so let's say n is,
like, let's say, 1,000,

00:24:25.010 --> 00:24:28.640
and let's say n
times P a is 200.

00:24:28.640 --> 00:24:34.100
So what is your expected
gap between consecutive a's?

00:24:34.100 --> 00:24:36.650
How far apart are
a is on average?

00:24:36.650 --> 00:24:41.780
If you have, say you have 1,000
total, and you have 200 a's.

00:24:45.810 --> 00:24:47.100
Anyone, anybody?

00:24:50.720 --> 00:24:51.725
How many think it's 4?

00:24:54.440 --> 00:24:55.960
5?

00:24:55.960 --> 00:24:56.720
OK, I hear 5.

00:24:56.720 --> 00:24:57.220
Good.

00:24:57.220 --> 00:24:58.870
So 5 is 1,000 by 200.

00:24:58.870 --> 00:25:02.320
This is just, like,
counting type arguments.

00:25:04.785 --> 00:25:07.160
Right, because we have 1,000
things and you have 200 a's,

00:25:07.160 --> 00:25:09.540
so on average, they're
like five apart, roughly.

00:25:09.540 --> 00:25:10.830
OK.

00:25:10.830 --> 00:25:18.860
So, really, the average spacing
in 1 over P a, n over n--

00:25:18.860 --> 00:25:21.620
n times P a, so 1 over P a
is the average gap between

00:25:21.620 --> 00:25:25.010
consecutive a's.

00:25:25.010 --> 00:25:26.750
So now, we have this.

00:25:26.750 --> 00:25:31.280
We will take a very giant
leap, and let's not read this,

00:25:31.280 --> 00:25:35.960
I just pasted it, but really
read this second part.

00:25:35.960 --> 00:25:40.470
So if you have
some sequence, x 0

00:25:40.470 --> 00:25:44.640
to n minus 1, the same
sequence, x 0 to n minus 1 also

00:25:44.640 --> 00:25:47.490
occurred roughly these
many positions ago.

00:25:47.490 --> 00:25:52.890
So, we were just talking about
iid and the single symbol.

00:25:52.890 --> 00:25:55.140
But you can extend
this to sequences

00:25:55.140 --> 00:25:56.940
in a stationary distribution.

00:25:56.940 --> 00:25:59.730
You can actually show
with this Kac's lemma

00:25:59.730 --> 00:26:04.170
that if you see some
sequence of length n now,

00:26:04.170 --> 00:26:06.750
the average sort of time
before-- like, last time you

00:26:06.750 --> 00:26:09.300
saw the sequence is
actually 1 over probability

00:26:09.300 --> 00:26:10.620
of that sequence.

00:26:10.620 --> 00:26:13.050
It's like a straightforward
extension of, like, a,

00:26:13.050 --> 00:26:17.370
you saw one by P a ago, x
n, you saw one by p x n ago.

00:26:17.370 --> 00:26:22.590
That sort of like the idea here.

00:26:22.590 --> 00:26:25.110
And now, if you
connect it to LZ77,

00:26:25.110 --> 00:26:29.040
this roughly means that
the match offset in LZ77

00:26:29.040 --> 00:26:32.055
is 1 over p of x n, right?

00:26:32.055 --> 00:26:33.180
Because this is the offset.

00:26:33.180 --> 00:26:34.620
You found a match basically.

00:26:34.620 --> 00:26:36.270
You are likely to
find the match, like,

00:26:36.270 --> 00:26:39.980
these many symbols ago.

00:26:39.980 --> 00:26:42.500
So any questions so far?

00:26:42.500 --> 00:26:44.180
This is very high
level proof, right,

00:26:44.180 --> 00:26:47.180
like the actual proof will have
many nuances and so on, which

00:26:47.180 --> 00:26:49.790
we'll not try to cover here.

00:26:49.790 --> 00:26:53.960
It's in the [INAUDIBLE] book,
it's in the EE 376C notes

00:26:53.960 --> 00:26:56.720
and so on.

00:26:56.720 --> 00:26:58.130
For now, this will suffice.

00:27:01.450 --> 00:27:04.900
Actually-- OK, actually this.

00:27:04.900 --> 00:27:08.050
So now, if you have an integer,
which is like 1 over p x n,

00:27:08.050 --> 00:27:10.660
do you remember like if
you have an integer n,

00:27:10.660 --> 00:27:12.460
then you can use like
a fixed length code,

00:27:12.460 --> 00:27:14.860
roughly encode it in
log n bits, right,

00:27:14.860 --> 00:27:17.860
like the usual thing where
you can encode an integer

00:27:17.860 --> 00:27:20.640
n with log n bits.

00:27:20.640 --> 00:27:25.980
So similarly, you can encode
this match offset, 1 over p,

00:27:25.980 --> 00:27:30.480
with roughly log 1 over p bits.

00:27:30.480 --> 00:27:32.655
What does this log 1
over p remind you of?

00:27:37.270 --> 00:27:39.250
Any rule that we
have been talking

00:27:39.250 --> 00:27:41.950
about from the second
lecture, basically, the thumb

00:27:41.950 --> 00:27:47.150
rule of compression that if you
can achieve log 1 over p bits,

00:27:47.150 --> 00:27:49.280
you are basically optimal.

00:27:49.280 --> 00:27:51.866
Right?

00:27:51.866 --> 00:27:56.700
So, minor note that you
can show relatively easily

00:27:56.700 --> 00:27:58.470
that the lengths and
literals don't really

00:27:58.470 --> 00:27:59.303
contribute anything.

00:27:59.303 --> 00:28:01.290
It's really the
offsets that take up,

00:28:01.290 --> 00:28:04.890
theoretically, all
of the space in LZ77.

00:28:04.890 --> 00:28:07.820
So we'll just focus
on the offsets.

00:28:07.820 --> 00:28:11.810
And then, expanding these many
bits, log 1 over p x n bits,

00:28:11.810 --> 00:28:13.880
means we are following
the thumb rule,

00:28:13.880 --> 00:28:17.270
and we use, on average, if
you take the expectation here,

00:28:17.270 --> 00:28:19.460
you get basically H
of X n, so therefore,

00:28:19.460 --> 00:28:22.550
for encoding n symbols,
you are using H of X n bits

00:28:22.550 --> 00:28:25.680
on average, which as we know,
is the optimal thing to do.

00:28:25.680 --> 00:28:27.020
You can't do better than that.

00:28:27.020 --> 00:28:29.120
And then you just
take n to the limit,

00:28:29.120 --> 00:28:31.690
and you get your entropy rate.

00:28:31.690 --> 00:28:34.915
So, good.

00:28:37.750 --> 00:28:40.480
So the idea is as
simple as a sequence

00:28:40.480 --> 00:28:44.530
is likely to be seen 1 over
p of that sequence before

00:28:44.530 --> 00:28:48.070
and therefore you can encode
it with log 1 over p bits,

00:28:48.070 --> 00:28:51.340
and therefore, you can
achieve the entropy rate.

00:28:51.340 --> 00:28:54.080
Right?

00:28:54.080 --> 00:28:54.710
Any questions?

00:28:54.710 --> 00:28:57.440
Is the high level sort
of flow of logic clear?

00:29:00.160 --> 00:29:04.570
If you are unhappy with
some of the details,

00:29:04.570 --> 00:29:06.980
you have a full right to do so.

00:29:06.980 --> 00:29:08.860
Maybe we can point
you to references

00:29:08.860 --> 00:29:11.080
if you want to look
at the actual proof.

00:29:13.940 --> 00:29:14.440
Yeah.

00:29:14.440 --> 00:29:15.910
So there are proofs.

00:29:15.910 --> 00:29:18.460
Sadly, the LZ77 proof,
even though the idea

00:29:18.460 --> 00:29:21.250
sounds very simple, it's
somewhat tricky to prove.

00:29:21.250 --> 00:29:26.110
LZ78 is much better
in terms of theory.

00:29:26.110 --> 00:29:29.170
But there is a reason we look
at this more because it's

00:29:29.170 --> 00:29:30.860
used much more in practice.

00:29:30.860 --> 00:29:33.160
So the proofs are not as
satisfying from a theory

00:29:33.160 --> 00:29:33.700
perspective.

00:29:33.700 --> 00:29:36.850
But this argument is like
the core of the proof

00:29:36.850 --> 00:29:39.630
and then you need to
do a lot of things

00:29:39.630 --> 00:29:42.360
to actually get there, which
I have not actually seen.

00:29:42.360 --> 00:29:43.980
So yeah.

00:29:46.610 --> 00:29:48.560
There's another reason,
like, the proof maybe

00:29:48.560 --> 00:29:50.540
is less important to
us in this lecture is

00:29:50.540 --> 00:29:56.970
that this asymptotic theory
doesn't fully explain

00:29:56.970 --> 00:29:58.590
how well it does in practice.

00:29:58.590 --> 00:30:00.090
The theory-- the
theoretical proofs

00:30:00.090 --> 00:30:03.710
are, like, somehow not
able to convince us

00:30:03.710 --> 00:30:06.140
that LZ77 should be so good.

00:30:06.140 --> 00:30:08.570
For a k-th order
Markov process, you

00:30:08.570 --> 00:30:11.060
can show that LZ77 will
achieve its entropy rate,

00:30:11.060 --> 00:30:12.710
the conditional
entropy in the limit.

00:30:12.710 --> 00:30:17.420
But for normal-sized data, you
don't expect it to do so well.

00:30:17.420 --> 00:30:18.570
There is a big--

00:30:18.570 --> 00:30:20.570
like, if you think of
terms of, like, the proof,

00:30:20.570 --> 00:30:22.940
right, if something is
converging to something,

00:30:22.940 --> 00:30:24.230
there is a convergence rate.

00:30:24.230 --> 00:30:26.540
How fast does it approach
the entropy rate,

00:30:26.540 --> 00:30:29.450
and the convergence
rates are not amazing.

00:30:29.450 --> 00:30:33.650
However, in practice, it
does very well on real data,

00:30:33.650 --> 00:30:35.900
not on these k-th
order Markov processes.

00:30:35.900 --> 00:30:39.320
And reason is that the world is
not really a k-th order Markov

00:30:39.320 --> 00:30:39.830
process.

00:30:39.830 --> 00:30:42.320
Your English text,
you see far longer

00:30:42.320 --> 00:30:43.880
repeats than you
would ever imagine

00:30:43.880 --> 00:30:45.890
seeing with a k-th
order Markov process.

00:30:45.890 --> 00:30:48.050
English is much
more complicated.

00:30:48.050 --> 00:30:51.440
Like a person's name in a text
will repeat so many times.

00:30:51.440 --> 00:30:53.960
If you are trying to model
it with a k-th order Markov

00:30:53.960 --> 00:30:57.830
process, you won't be really
able to model it very well.

00:30:57.830 --> 00:31:00.380
So that's why, like,
we saw last time,

00:31:00.380 --> 00:31:04.350
this simplistic k-th order
models and so on, and the LLMs

00:31:04.350 --> 00:31:07.830
just, like, beat them
by a wide, wide margin

00:31:07.830 --> 00:31:11.520
because they are able to
model it in a much better way

00:31:11.520 --> 00:31:14.385
than any of these small
order models can do.

00:31:20.500 --> 00:31:23.525
Of course, in terms of compute,
that's a different story.

00:31:23.525 --> 00:31:24.025
OK.

00:31:26.720 --> 00:31:29.450
So that's it for the proof.

00:31:29.450 --> 00:31:34.010
We will now look at some
practical aspects of LZ77.

00:31:34.010 --> 00:31:38.480
And I do want to spend some time
on practical tips which is new

00:31:38.480 --> 00:31:39.110
this year.

00:31:39.110 --> 00:31:39.610
OK.

00:31:43.870 --> 00:31:46.060
So what we did, as
we have been doing,

00:31:46.060 --> 00:31:48.550
is we implemented
LZ77 in SCL, so we

00:31:48.550 --> 00:31:50.050
had an implementation
from last year

00:31:50.050 --> 00:31:54.340
and then, yesterday, night
time, I implemented another one

00:31:54.340 --> 00:31:56.230
in the last couple of days.

00:31:56.230 --> 00:32:00.075
So we will look at
some things about how

00:32:00.075 --> 00:32:01.450
the matches look
in practice, how

00:32:01.450 --> 00:32:02.700
the match lengths and offsets.

00:32:02.700 --> 00:32:05.620
Of course, this year, with
ChatGPT, the code-writing part

00:32:05.620 --> 00:32:07.510
became much easier.

00:32:07.510 --> 00:32:09.280
Anyway.

00:32:09.280 --> 00:32:13.280
So, I don't know if
anybody identifies this.

00:32:13.280 --> 00:32:17.660
This Is Alice in Wonderland
which is in one of the text,

00:32:17.660 --> 00:32:19.700
like, the standard
compression corpuses.

00:32:19.700 --> 00:32:21.770
So I just took it.

00:32:21.770 --> 00:32:24.260
And see, this is a match.

00:32:24.260 --> 00:32:27.057
So this sort of match, like, a
k-th order model won't explain.

00:32:27.057 --> 00:32:28.640
Like, this is a very
long, long match.

00:32:28.640 --> 00:32:32.570
Beautiful soup, beautiful
soup, soup of the evening.

00:32:32.570 --> 00:32:34.670
So yeah.

00:32:34.670 --> 00:32:38.390
You see the selected text
exactly matches between the two

00:32:38.390 --> 00:32:40.190
paragraphs, right?

00:32:40.190 --> 00:32:41.210
LZ77 would just kill it.

00:32:41.210 --> 00:32:41.900
Like, think of it.

00:32:41.900 --> 00:32:42.170
Right?

00:32:42.170 --> 00:32:44.750
Each of these will take you
some number of bits to describe.

00:32:44.750 --> 00:32:48.020
But LZ77, all of this it would
describe in just two numbers--

00:32:48.020 --> 00:32:49.740
the offset and the length.

00:32:49.740 --> 00:32:50.240
Right?

00:32:50.240 --> 00:32:51.470
So that's the whole idea.

00:32:51.470 --> 00:32:53.210
Like, the text--
after you parse it,

00:32:53.210 --> 00:32:55.610
it becomes so much smaller and--

00:32:55.610 --> 00:32:56.110
yeah.

00:33:02.090 --> 00:33:09.790
So this is, I think, from
a CSS file, I believe.

00:33:09.790 --> 00:33:12.740
Like, this web styling things.

00:33:12.740 --> 00:33:16.360
And here, also, you see
these very long matches,

00:33:16.360 --> 00:33:20.920
this transparent 25%,
transparent 25%, something.

00:33:20.920 --> 00:33:24.160
So again, very suitable
for LZ77 compression.

00:33:27.880 --> 00:33:29.890
But not always do you
find these long matches.

00:33:29.890 --> 00:33:31.720
Even short matches
are good, and we

00:33:31.720 --> 00:33:36.430
will do some quick math to make
sure that that makes sense.

00:33:36.430 --> 00:33:40.075
So, pleasure-- this word,
in this particular book,

00:33:40.075 --> 00:33:41.700
occurs-- like, the
first time it occurs

00:33:41.700 --> 00:33:46.020
and the second time it occurs
is like 150 kilobytes, 150,000

00:33:46.020 --> 00:33:47.340
bytes apart.

00:33:47.340 --> 00:33:50.070
And it's on the first
page of the book,

00:33:50.070 --> 00:33:51.720
and the last page of the book.

00:33:51.720 --> 00:33:56.490
So let's see if I can find it--

00:33:56.490 --> 00:34:00.880
"pleasure" here,
and "pleasure" here.

00:34:00.880 --> 00:34:01.530
Right?

00:34:01.530 --> 00:34:03.630
So now, let's do a
quick calculation, just

00:34:03.630 --> 00:34:06.930
a back of the envelope thing,
just to just to compute, like,

00:34:06.930 --> 00:34:09.179
is it worth it to
encode this as a match.

00:34:09.179 --> 00:34:15.860
So these are the
best compressors,

00:34:15.860 --> 00:34:19.510
spend roughly one bit per byte,
but like a usual Huffman code,

00:34:19.510 --> 00:34:20.510
I would spend much more.

00:34:20.510 --> 00:34:22.850
It would spend like let's
say three bits per byte,

00:34:22.850 --> 00:34:25.380
just the back of the envelope.

00:34:25.380 --> 00:34:26.969
And "pleasure" has how many?

00:34:26.969 --> 00:34:33.420
It has eight characters so you
would spend like 24 bits on it.

00:34:33.420 --> 00:34:35.639
Just doing a rough calculation.

00:34:35.639 --> 00:34:40.170
OK, now, you have one 50
kilobytes, which is what?

00:34:40.170 --> 00:34:43.719
150,000.

00:34:43.719 --> 00:34:46.480
So to describe it, you need
roughly these many bits

00:34:46.480 --> 00:34:47.619
log of this thing.

00:34:51.889 --> 00:34:53.710
Can anybody help me?

00:34:53.710 --> 00:34:55.219
Sorry.

00:34:55.219 --> 00:34:58.680
Calculate this, roughly.

00:34:58.680 --> 00:34:59.760
If you have a calculator?

00:35:07.230 --> 00:35:08.724
[INAUDIBLE]

00:35:08.724 --> 00:35:11.730
[INAUDIBLE]

00:35:11.730 --> 00:35:13.850
And make sure it's log base 2.

00:35:13.850 --> 00:35:16.410
It's like around 16.

00:35:16.410 --> 00:35:19.170
OK, somewhere in
the 14 to 17 range.

00:35:19.170 --> 00:35:20.610
[INAUDIBLE]

00:35:20.610 --> 00:35:21.170
17.

00:35:21.170 --> 00:35:22.260
[INAUDIBLE]

00:35:22.260 --> 00:35:23.700
17, good, OK.

00:35:23.700 --> 00:35:24.350
0.2.

00:35:24.350 --> 00:35:26.390
OK 0.2.

00:35:26.390 --> 00:35:26.890
Right.

00:35:26.890 --> 00:35:29.260
So you already see that
even this very short

00:35:29.260 --> 00:35:33.910
match at a very long distance,
that is still saving you bits

00:35:33.910 --> 00:35:34.658
by encoding it.

00:35:34.658 --> 00:35:36.700
And, like, these three
bits is not out of random,

00:35:36.700 --> 00:35:39.430
right, like this is the zeroth
order entropy of English,

00:35:39.430 --> 00:35:40.300
as we saw.

00:35:40.300 --> 00:35:42.700
I think it was actually
roughly around four bits.

00:35:42.700 --> 00:35:44.950
So, right.

00:35:44.950 --> 00:35:48.880
So you see the idea here is
that far away short matches

00:35:48.880 --> 00:35:51.850
even those are beneficial
because it's so much

00:35:51.850 --> 00:35:53.650
cheaper to encode this 150--

00:35:53.650 --> 00:35:56.380
just the length 150,000
as compared to encoding

00:35:56.380 --> 00:36:00.560
the P-L-E-A-S-U-R-E,
these eight characters.

00:36:00.560 --> 00:36:01.060
Right?

00:36:01.060 --> 00:36:04.848
And you can play with this
more, and actually see.

00:36:04.848 --> 00:36:06.640
There will be-- like,
as you might imagine,

00:36:06.640 --> 00:36:09.490
there will be a crossing
point where it just--

00:36:09.490 --> 00:36:12.940
if you have a match of length
two at a billion offset,

00:36:12.940 --> 00:36:14.470
that point, you
might say, OK, maybe

00:36:14.470 --> 00:36:15.670
it's not a worthwhile match.

00:36:15.670 --> 00:36:19.935
But still it is, in this case,
it is a worthwhile match.

00:36:19.935 --> 00:36:20.435
OK.

00:36:26.080 --> 00:36:30.190
And we will just gather
the data, try to look at,

00:36:30.190 --> 00:36:33.730
like, what is the
distribution of match offsets

00:36:33.730 --> 00:36:36.100
and match lengths after
you do this LZ77 parsing

00:36:36.100 --> 00:36:38.330
for a particular
implementation of LZ77.

00:36:38.330 --> 00:36:41.560
We will see it's not
like a unique thing.

00:36:41.560 --> 00:36:44.380
So you see, mostly you
have smaller offsets,

00:36:44.380 --> 00:36:46.240
and sometimes, you
have bigger offsets.

00:36:46.240 --> 00:36:49.130
So it's an exponential scale.

00:36:49.130 --> 00:36:49.630
Right?

00:36:49.630 --> 00:36:52.570
So in this case, the longest
offsets are around 150,000,

00:36:52.570 --> 00:36:56.050
because the text itself
is around 150,000 long.

00:36:56.050 --> 00:36:57.490
And in terms of
match lengths, you

00:36:57.490 --> 00:36:59.920
see, oftentimes, you see
relatively small match lengths.

00:36:59.920 --> 00:37:04.450
Once in a while, you see match
lengths more than 100 bytes.

00:37:04.450 --> 00:37:05.680
Right?

00:37:05.680 --> 00:37:08.230
This is very important
to understand that this

00:37:08.230 --> 00:37:09.460
is for this particular file.

00:37:09.460 --> 00:37:11.800
From file to file, from
data set to data set,

00:37:11.800 --> 00:37:13.570
this distribution
looks different.

00:37:13.570 --> 00:37:17.950
And that should influence
what particular LZ77 technique

00:37:17.950 --> 00:37:19.555
you use, as we'll see shortly.

00:37:22.590 --> 00:37:25.650
This is the CSS
file we saw earlier,

00:37:25.650 --> 00:37:29.340
the one with the percent,
50%, that sort of thing.

00:37:29.340 --> 00:37:34.550
Bootstrap min.css-- match
offset maybe looks similar.

00:37:34.550 --> 00:37:36.035
Match lengths, you
will notice here

00:37:36.035 --> 00:37:37.160
something different, right?

00:37:37.160 --> 00:37:39.080
There are some
extremely long matches,

00:37:39.080 --> 00:37:42.470
more than like 500 bytes long.

00:37:42.470 --> 00:37:47.370
So you can do this for any
file and just play with it,

00:37:47.370 --> 00:37:48.510
try to understand.

00:37:48.510 --> 00:37:52.850
Like if you imagine
structured pieces of data,

00:37:52.850 --> 00:37:55.250
like code, for example,
would have a lot of matches

00:37:55.250 --> 00:37:57.920
of the exactly same type.

00:37:57.920 --> 00:38:00.540
Because the same keywords
occur many, many times.

00:38:00.540 --> 00:38:01.040
Right?

00:38:01.040 --> 00:38:03.320
The same variable names,
the same function names.

00:38:03.320 --> 00:38:06.080
So that distribution will look
different, compared to text.

00:38:06.080 --> 00:38:08.270
Where you have repeated
words, but maybe not,

00:38:08.270 --> 00:38:11.900
like, repeated
sentences exactly.

00:38:11.900 --> 00:38:15.380
If you have a table, like a CSV
file, maybe some of the rows

00:38:15.380 --> 00:38:16.850
are exact matches of each other.

00:38:16.850 --> 00:38:20.600
So again, that's
different from text.

00:38:20.600 --> 00:38:23.750
If you have random data, maybe
you don't find any matches,

00:38:23.750 --> 00:38:25.580
or you find very few matches.

00:38:25.580 --> 00:38:28.940
So this will be different
for different data sets.

00:38:31.580 --> 00:38:35.510
OK, any questions so far?

00:38:35.510 --> 00:38:36.760
Right.

00:38:36.760 --> 00:38:41.290
So just want to say one thing
that LZ77 is, like, I think,

00:38:41.290 --> 00:38:43.900
today, the most popular
compression technique.

00:38:43.900 --> 00:38:46.180
It's the one that's most
widely used in practice.

00:38:46.180 --> 00:38:48.940
To do full justice
to it, to explain

00:38:48.940 --> 00:38:51.717
even like how gzip works would
take me several lectures,

00:38:51.717 --> 00:38:53.800
and to explain how something
like Zstandard, which

00:38:53.800 --> 00:38:55.840
is a modern version
works would probably

00:38:55.840 --> 00:38:57.490
take me the entire course.

00:38:57.490 --> 00:39:00.850
Plus I don't think I'm
qualified to actually talk

00:39:00.850 --> 00:39:02.320
so much about LZ77.

00:39:02.320 --> 00:39:06.700
But we will try to look at
some practical aspects of LZ77,

00:39:06.700 --> 00:39:09.910
things that I'm more
confident to speak about

00:39:09.910 --> 00:39:12.670
without bringing in, like, the
creator of these algorithms.

00:39:12.670 --> 00:39:13.300
OK.

00:39:13.300 --> 00:39:16.343
So first thing,
there's this whole idea

00:39:16.343 --> 00:39:17.260
is very simple, right?

00:39:17.260 --> 00:39:20.410
But do I need to keep
this infinite past memory?

00:39:20.410 --> 00:39:23.860
Do I need to keep all of
the past, at every time,

00:39:23.860 --> 00:39:25.030
to find matches?

00:39:25.030 --> 00:39:26.950
Like if I have a
1 gigabyte file,

00:39:26.950 --> 00:39:29.740
do I need to keep the
last gigabyte in my memory

00:39:29.740 --> 00:39:32.320
and then look for matches
in that last one gigabyte?

00:39:32.320 --> 00:39:35.830
That doesn't sound efficient
and people don't do that.

00:39:35.830 --> 00:39:39.640
What people do is use something
called a sliding window LZ77

00:39:39.640 --> 00:39:41.590
where you only try
to find matches

00:39:41.590 --> 00:39:43.750
in the last 10
kilobytes or a few

00:39:43.750 --> 00:39:45.820
megabytes, that sort of thing.

00:39:45.820 --> 00:39:47.110
So you limit the scope.

00:39:47.110 --> 00:39:49.960
You don't look for matches
that are arbitrarily far away.

00:39:49.960 --> 00:39:52.150
So that's one thing.

00:39:52.150 --> 00:39:53.920
And some technical
details, like, you

00:39:53.920 --> 00:39:57.460
can use circular buffers,
some particular techniques

00:39:57.460 --> 00:40:00.190
to efficiently handle windows
without reallocating memory

00:40:00.190 --> 00:40:02.395
every time, or moving a
bunch of data every time.

00:40:04.970 --> 00:40:08.322
And we'll see this
one in the next slide.

00:40:08.322 --> 00:40:10.280
That bigger windows give
you better compression

00:40:10.280 --> 00:40:12.115
because now you have more--

00:40:12.115 --> 00:40:14.240
basically, it's just making
the compression encoder

00:40:14.240 --> 00:40:15.200
more powerful.

00:40:15.200 --> 00:40:18.350
Like it can find
faraway matches.

00:40:18.350 --> 00:40:20.470
But it needs more
memory because then you

00:40:20.470 --> 00:40:22.220
need to store that
entire window with you,

00:40:22.220 --> 00:40:25.850
both in the compression
and the decompression.

00:40:25.850 --> 00:40:29.390
We have implemented this in SCL.

00:40:29.390 --> 00:40:30.800
I encourage you to look at it.

00:40:30.800 --> 00:40:32.150
It's, I think, well-documented.

00:40:32.150 --> 00:40:35.750
And I had a hard
time like actually

00:40:35.750 --> 00:40:39.290
finding an implementation
that was easy to explain.

00:40:39.290 --> 00:40:42.080
So I hope this will be
useful, especially if you

00:40:42.080 --> 00:40:44.420
are doing a project on this.

00:40:44.420 --> 00:40:49.200
I will mention some specific
project ideas shortly.

00:40:49.200 --> 00:40:51.240
So I took the Alice
in Wonderland book.

00:40:51.240 --> 00:40:55.800
I increased the window size
in an exponential scale,

00:40:55.800 --> 00:40:59.570
and I looked at the
compressed size in bytes.

00:40:59.570 --> 00:41:01.880
So what you see is that
as you expect, right?

00:41:01.880 --> 00:41:06.280
As you improve the window,
you get better and better

00:41:06.280 --> 00:41:07.300
compression.

00:41:07.300 --> 00:41:08.952
At some point, it
starts leveling off.

00:41:08.952 --> 00:41:11.410
In this case, because the file
itself has a certain length,

00:41:11.410 --> 00:41:13.327
so making the window
bigger than the file size

00:41:13.327 --> 00:41:14.620
obviously is not going to help.

00:41:14.620 --> 00:41:16.298
But even in general,
after a point,

00:41:16.298 --> 00:41:17.965
it doesn't help to
have a bigger window.

00:41:20.210 --> 00:41:22.210
Again, this is something
which is data-specific.

00:41:22.210 --> 00:41:24.400
For your data, may be
bigger windows are better.

00:41:24.400 --> 00:41:27.490
Or for your application, maybe
you can afford a lot of memory.

00:41:27.490 --> 00:41:30.130
So it's all about
what you want to do.

00:41:30.130 --> 00:41:33.820
There are no hard rules that
always use 100,000 windows.

00:41:33.820 --> 00:41:35.380
That's not the
lesson of this plot.

00:41:40.190 --> 00:41:41.210
Another question.

00:41:41.210 --> 00:41:43.040
The basic question, right?

00:41:43.040 --> 00:41:46.190
We did it by hand,
by eye earlier.

00:41:46.190 --> 00:41:50.590
But clearly, like, a
computer won't do that.

00:41:50.590 --> 00:41:52.090
How to find matches?

00:41:52.090 --> 00:41:54.340
Match-finding, this
is like the core

00:41:54.340 --> 00:41:56.350
of an encoder, not the decoder.

00:41:56.350 --> 00:41:57.730
This is important to understand.

00:41:57.730 --> 00:41:59.200
No matter how you
find the matches,

00:41:59.200 --> 00:42:01.150
the decoder can still
decode your data.

00:42:01.150 --> 00:42:04.690
It's an encoder-only property,
this match-finding business.

00:42:04.690 --> 00:42:06.940
How do I find a
match in the past?

00:42:06.940 --> 00:42:12.070
And the basic idea, which again,
you can find the code, is to--

00:42:16.630 --> 00:42:19.500
what you do
basically is that you

00:42:19.500 --> 00:42:22.200
take all of these
different common patterns,

00:42:22.200 --> 00:42:25.020
like the patterns that
you have seen in the past,

00:42:25.020 --> 00:42:28.500
and you say that A B C D
has occurred at position 1,

00:42:28.500 --> 00:42:33.150
position 10, position 15,
position 20, let's say.

00:42:33.150 --> 00:42:35.400
And this guy has
occurred at position 2

00:42:35.400 --> 00:42:38.010
and position 14 or something.

00:42:38.010 --> 00:42:40.260
So you make this
big table where each

00:42:40.260 --> 00:42:42.300
of these four-length
sequences, when have they

00:42:42.300 --> 00:42:43.620
occurred in the past?

00:42:43.620 --> 00:42:49.420
And now, you are at some point
in the file you see an A B C D.

00:42:49.420 --> 00:42:53.140
So you see, oh, I have seen
A B C D, past occurrences,

00:42:53.140 --> 00:42:53.890
basically.

00:42:57.000 --> 00:43:00.730
Are at 1, 10, 15, 20.

00:43:00.730 --> 00:43:01.230
Right?

00:43:01.230 --> 00:43:05.740
Because you store this in
a hash table, basically.

00:43:05.740 --> 00:43:07.690
So you store this
table, which is

00:43:07.690 --> 00:43:09.760
like for every
four-length sequence, when

00:43:09.760 --> 00:43:13.940
have I seen it in the past
for all possible sequences?

00:43:13.940 --> 00:43:16.640
And then, when you find
the sequence in the future,

00:43:16.640 --> 00:43:18.380
you look at its
past occurrences.

00:43:18.380 --> 00:43:20.065
And you try to see
which one of them

00:43:20.065 --> 00:43:21.440
would give you
the longest match.

00:43:21.440 --> 00:43:26.180
So maybe you saw A B C D at,
like, position 10 in the past,

00:43:26.180 --> 00:43:30.680
and at position 1 in the past.

00:43:30.680 --> 00:43:33.890
However, in this case this was
followed by E F. In this case,

00:43:33.890 --> 00:43:36.020
this was followed by, like, ZX.

00:43:36.020 --> 00:43:39.180
And here, it's followed
by E F. So you would

00:43:39.180 --> 00:43:41.520
prefer the first one, right?

00:43:41.520 --> 00:43:42.750
This seems a better match.

00:43:42.750 --> 00:43:47.500
This is a longer match
instead of this one.

00:43:47.500 --> 00:43:49.510
So you have a lot of
candidate positions

00:43:49.510 --> 00:43:52.180
where this A B C D occurred
and then in the past,

00:43:52.180 --> 00:43:55.810
you look at where did you find
the longer match to this A B C

00:43:55.810 --> 00:43:57.010
D E F.

00:43:57.010 --> 00:44:00.890
So this is like a basic
idea commonly used

00:44:00.890 --> 00:44:03.230
in many algorithms, like
a seed-type idea, where

00:44:03.230 --> 00:44:05.270
to find a match,
what you do is you

00:44:05.270 --> 00:44:08.390
take a four-length, or a
three-length, five-length seed.

00:44:08.390 --> 00:44:11.120
Seed is the thing you
index in your hash table.

00:44:11.120 --> 00:44:13.430
So in this case, it's all
four-length sequences that

00:44:13.430 --> 00:44:15.020
are hashed in the sequence.

00:44:15.020 --> 00:44:17.480
You use that to find
candidate matches.

00:44:17.480 --> 00:44:21.200
And then you find the best match
out of those candidate matches.

00:44:21.200 --> 00:44:23.210
So really, like,
reduces the search space

00:44:23.210 --> 00:44:25.010
when you're looking for things.

00:44:25.010 --> 00:44:29.300
So index past occurrences of
sequences in a data structure,

00:44:29.300 --> 00:44:33.080
like hash table, binary tree,
you can choose your favorite.

00:44:33.080 --> 00:44:35.300
And for the given
position, do a lookup

00:44:35.300 --> 00:44:39.170
to find previous occurrences and
then extend the candidate match

00:44:39.170 --> 00:44:40.292
to find the longest match.

00:44:40.292 --> 00:44:41.750
So you extend it
in the future, you

00:44:41.750 --> 00:44:43.505
see how long does
it keep matching.

00:44:47.660 --> 00:44:48.260
Again--

00:44:48.260 --> 00:44:49.160
[INAUDIBLE]

00:44:49.160 --> 00:44:51.322
Yep.

00:44:51.322 --> 00:44:54.080
Do we just consider
not worth it matching

00:44:54.080 --> 00:44:57.910
two or three substrates then?

00:44:57.910 --> 00:45:01.390
So you are asking why I took
four instead of two or three?

00:45:01.390 --> 00:45:01.900
Yeah.

00:45:01.900 --> 00:45:03.160
Yes, that's a good question.

00:45:06.770 --> 00:45:09.560
There is a trade-off there,
which we'll see shortly.

00:45:09.560 --> 00:45:14.530
But just to answer
the specific thing,

00:45:14.530 --> 00:45:17.650
a two-length thing would give
you a lot of false positives.

00:45:17.650 --> 00:45:20.845
Because you have seen A B much
more than you have seen the A B

00:45:20.845 --> 00:45:22.120
C D whole thing, right?

00:45:22.120 --> 00:45:25.300
So now, if you are just indexing
two length or three length,

00:45:25.300 --> 00:45:27.790
you will find many
more possible things.

00:45:27.790 --> 00:45:30.310
So it will increase your
complexity quite a bit

00:45:30.310 --> 00:45:33.730
just to find all
of the possible--

00:45:33.730 --> 00:45:35.440
to find the longest one.

00:45:35.440 --> 00:45:37.283
On the other hand, if
you really work hard,

00:45:37.283 --> 00:45:39.700
you will actually find maybe
a better match than you would

00:45:39.700 --> 00:45:41.270
find with a four-length thing.

00:45:41.270 --> 00:45:42.970
So that's the
trade-off, basically.

00:45:42.970 --> 00:45:46.750
It will often make you slower,
but may be better compression.

00:45:46.750 --> 00:45:48.670
But in practice, we will
see when that is not

00:45:48.670 --> 00:45:50.050
true for a particular reason.

00:45:53.210 --> 00:45:55.310
But yes, if you make it
too big, that's very bad.

00:45:55.310 --> 00:45:57.435
Because then you will miss
out on a lot of matches.

00:46:01.730 --> 00:46:06.750
OK, another sort of thing
that's not immediately obvious

00:46:06.750 --> 00:46:10.560
is that the LZ77
that we presented,

00:46:10.560 --> 00:46:12.620
like, the theoretical
version that is greedy.

00:46:12.620 --> 00:46:16.280
Greedy means that the
first time it sees a match,

00:46:16.280 --> 00:46:17.630
it picks up that match.

00:46:17.630 --> 00:46:19.130
It doesn't think
of the future, it

00:46:19.130 --> 00:46:22.490
doesn't try to do some complex
optimization problem where,

00:46:22.490 --> 00:46:24.200
oh, maybe I should
not take this match,

00:46:24.200 --> 00:46:26.300
maybe I should take a
longer match in the future.

00:46:26.300 --> 00:46:27.890
Let me give you an example.

00:46:27.890 --> 00:46:30.190
I'll make it very clear.

00:46:30.190 --> 00:46:48.500
So, OK.

00:46:48.500 --> 00:46:50.500
So let's say this is your
sequence at some point

00:46:50.500 --> 00:46:55.310
in the past you saw a B C D E F.
I'd say B B C E D F A B E F A B

00:46:55.310 --> 00:46:57.980
C D. OK, so now you're here.

00:46:57.980 --> 00:47:00.130
So the algorithm we
were just discussing,

00:47:00.130 --> 00:47:03.100
you would see an A B. You will
say, oh I found a match to A B,

00:47:03.100 --> 00:47:04.540
and you will say, good.

00:47:04.540 --> 00:47:05.210
I'm done.

00:47:05.210 --> 00:47:05.710
Right?

00:47:08.430 --> 00:47:11.100
However, maybe you
can be smarter.

00:47:11.100 --> 00:47:13.380
You might say, OK, maybe
I saw this match of A B,

00:47:13.380 --> 00:47:14.280
but it's too short.

00:47:14.280 --> 00:47:15.690
It's just of length two.

00:47:15.690 --> 00:47:17.070
What if I skipped A?

00:47:17.070 --> 00:47:21.510
Let me skip A. Let me try to
find a match starting at B.

00:47:21.510 --> 00:47:24.870
And suddenly, I find
a very long match.

00:47:24.870 --> 00:47:27.450
A match of length five
instead of length two.

00:47:27.450 --> 00:47:28.170
Right?

00:47:28.170 --> 00:47:29.730
So the first one is greedy.

00:47:29.730 --> 00:47:32.250
This one is, like--
the first one is greedy

00:47:32.250 --> 00:47:37.340
because it just takes the first
thing and says, OK, that's it.

00:47:37.340 --> 00:47:38.900
The second one is lazy.

00:47:38.900 --> 00:47:42.770
Lazy in the sense that it says,
OK, maybe I found a match,

00:47:42.770 --> 00:47:44.060
but let's not get too excited.

00:47:44.060 --> 00:47:45.410
Let's look a bit in the future.

00:47:45.410 --> 00:47:46.822
Maybe I will find
a better match.

00:47:46.822 --> 00:47:48.155
Let's not pick it up right away.

00:47:50.980 --> 00:47:51.855
Does that make sense?

00:47:54.455 --> 00:47:54.955
Right?

00:47:59.600 --> 00:48:04.460
And as you can imagine,
the real, like--

00:48:04.460 --> 00:48:07.730
people who have looked into
this, didn't stop at this lazy.

00:48:07.730 --> 00:48:10.970
This seems like, OK, but how do
you exactly do the lazy thing?

00:48:10.970 --> 00:48:13.580
They said, OK, let's make it
into an optimization problem.

00:48:13.580 --> 00:48:15.740
Let's solve a perfect
optimization problem.

00:48:15.740 --> 00:48:18.210
Let's make a dynamic
program out of it.

00:48:18.210 --> 00:48:20.650
And you can do a lot of things.

00:48:20.650 --> 00:48:22.400
There is something
called optimal parsing.

00:48:22.400 --> 00:48:24.050
There is a big theory
around it where

00:48:24.050 --> 00:48:26.720
what is the LZ77
parsing that gives you

00:48:26.720 --> 00:48:28.790
the fewest number
of literals, or that

00:48:28.790 --> 00:48:30.320
gives you the best compression?

00:48:30.320 --> 00:48:33.080
But they just get progressively
slower and slower.

00:48:33.080 --> 00:48:34.910
The more complex
their strategy is.

00:48:34.910 --> 00:48:37.700
So you can imagine greedy is
very fast because you just

00:48:37.700 --> 00:48:39.110
take the first match.

00:48:39.110 --> 00:48:41.570
Lazy is slower because you
don't take sometimes matches,

00:48:41.570 --> 00:48:44.120
then you try-- in the future,
try to find a longer match.

00:48:44.120 --> 00:48:47.040
And leave some
things as literals.

00:48:47.040 --> 00:48:49.860
Is this concept clear,
roughly, that sometimes being

00:48:49.860 --> 00:48:51.540
greedy is not the best option?

00:48:51.540 --> 00:48:53.220
Sometimes you want
to be a bit lazy,

00:48:53.220 --> 00:48:55.380
see if there is a better
match in the future.

00:49:00.180 --> 00:49:05.310
OK, so just to show you
what a typical encoder would

00:49:05.310 --> 00:49:09.360
be structured like, so
this is from the SCL class,

00:49:09.360 --> 00:49:11.870
sliding window encoder.

00:49:11.870 --> 00:49:15.290
So the encoder would take a
match finder which basically

00:49:15.290 --> 00:49:19.700
gives you the matches, and it
would take a window size, which

00:49:19.700 --> 00:49:23.370
is the size of the window, and
then the match finder would

00:49:23.370 --> 00:49:26.070
have some way to extend matches,
but, really, the main function

00:49:26.070 --> 00:49:28.650
is this, find_best_match,
where given some sequence,

00:49:28.650 --> 00:49:30.480
it will try to find
the best match.

00:49:30.480 --> 00:49:32.220
And there can be
various implementation.

00:49:32.220 --> 00:49:33.970
You could have a lazy
thing, greedy thing,

00:49:33.970 --> 00:49:38.190
just a bunch of different
ideas there-- optimal thing.

00:49:38.190 --> 00:49:40.740
And then this particular
match finder we implemented,

00:49:40.740 --> 00:49:43.380
you see it has so many
parameters-- hash_length,

00:49:43.380 --> 00:49:46.200
which is four in the
example we just--

00:49:46.200 --> 00:49:47.580
we were talking about.

00:49:47.580 --> 00:49:50.400
There's a hash_table_size
because you don't want

00:49:50.400 --> 00:49:53.190
an arbitrary large hash table.

00:49:53.190 --> 00:49:56.550
Something on max_chain_length,
which we will not talk about.

00:49:56.550 --> 00:49:58.920
Whether you are lazy or
whether you are greedy.

00:49:58.920 --> 00:50:02.100
Is there a minimum match length?

00:50:02.100 --> 00:50:05.730
So this is just like an
implementation I did.

00:50:05.730 --> 00:50:07.800
If you look at
Zstandard or gzip,

00:50:07.800 --> 00:50:12.260
they have more
complexities around it.

00:50:12.260 --> 00:50:14.080
But you can imagine that--

00:50:14.080 --> 00:50:16.040
the really nice thing
is that all of these

00:50:16.040 --> 00:50:17.570
are compliant with
the same decoder.

00:50:17.570 --> 00:50:18.860
The decoder is not
changing, right?

00:50:18.860 --> 00:50:20.090
We are just playing
with the encoder,

00:50:20.090 --> 00:50:22.520
trying to find a better and
better parsing of the data

00:50:22.520 --> 00:50:24.260
while using the best decoder.

00:50:24.260 --> 00:50:26.900
And that's a very nice idea,
because that allows you to--

00:50:26.900 --> 00:50:29.510
without breaking your
consumers, people

00:50:29.510 --> 00:50:31.010
who are reading
your files, they can

00:50:31.010 --> 00:50:32.480
keep using the same decoder.

00:50:32.480 --> 00:50:34.700
But you just get better
and better compression

00:50:34.700 --> 00:50:38.580
by using these fancy
complex strategies.

00:50:38.580 --> 00:50:39.080
OK.

00:50:39.080 --> 00:50:41.850
Are we happy?

00:50:41.850 --> 00:50:43.335
Or at least satisfied?

00:50:43.335 --> 00:50:43.835
OK.

00:50:51.560 --> 00:50:53.297
So, this is like
an experiment I did

00:50:53.297 --> 00:50:55.880
where I had something called a
minimum match length, which is,

00:50:55.880 --> 00:50:58.430
like, what is the shortest
match I'm going to allow.

00:50:58.430 --> 00:51:01.610
And I kept increasing the
minimum match length for,

00:51:01.610 --> 00:51:03.820
again, this Alice
In Wonderland book.

00:51:03.820 --> 00:51:06.250
And what I'm showing here
is that as I increase

00:51:06.250 --> 00:51:08.890
the minimum match length, I
get more and more literals

00:51:08.890 --> 00:51:11.020
but fewer and fewer matches.

00:51:11.020 --> 00:51:14.320
Can somebody roughly explain
why that might be happening?

00:51:14.320 --> 00:51:15.970
So I'm basically
rejecting any match

00:51:15.970 --> 00:51:18.205
that's shorter than a
particular match length.

00:51:21.780 --> 00:51:24.915
Why would I say more literals
as I increase my match length?

00:51:30.792 --> 00:51:33.720
The match then the
store is moving.

00:51:33.720 --> 00:51:34.440
Yeah.

00:51:34.440 --> 00:51:35.670
The answer is, if you
don't have a match,

00:51:35.670 --> 00:51:36.870
you have to store it
as a literal, right?

00:51:36.870 --> 00:51:38.760
And now you're disallowing
a lot of matches.

00:51:38.760 --> 00:51:41.010
Any short sequence you
don't even allow as a match.

00:51:41.010 --> 00:51:44.580
So therefore, you just get
much more unmatched literals.

00:51:44.580 --> 00:51:47.250
And therefore, you pay less to
store the matches because there

00:51:47.250 --> 00:51:48.540
are just fewer matches, right?

00:51:48.540 --> 00:51:51.120
You only take very long matches
you say no short matches are

00:51:51.120 --> 00:51:52.800
not something I care about.

00:51:52.800 --> 00:51:55.620
However, you see there is a
trade-off now because you get

00:51:55.620 --> 00:51:58.710
more and more literals,
fewer and fewer--

00:51:58.710 --> 00:52:01.020
so there is, like, some
sort of middle ground

00:52:01.020 --> 00:52:04.050
where you don't want your
minimum match to be too small.

00:52:04.050 --> 00:52:07.300
If it's too small, literals--

00:52:07.300 --> 00:52:09.800
everything matches, but then
you have just too many matches.

00:52:09.800 --> 00:52:12.258
You have too many matches of,
like, length one, length two.

00:52:12.258 --> 00:52:14.390
And those matches cost a lot.

00:52:14.390 --> 00:52:16.970
Maybe it's better to not have
these super small matches.

00:52:16.970 --> 00:52:19.610
Instead use a bigger match.

00:52:19.610 --> 00:52:21.500
And leave a few literals.

00:52:21.500 --> 00:52:25.265
So there is a trade-off
as in a lot of things.

00:52:31.830 --> 00:52:33.330
And then this is
another thing I did

00:52:33.330 --> 00:52:36.120
where I looked at the lazy
and the greedy strategy,

00:52:36.120 --> 00:52:37.920
again, for this particular file.

00:52:37.920 --> 00:52:40.320
Where the greedy strategy
just picks the first match

00:52:40.320 --> 00:52:43.050
it finds of that
minimum match length.

00:52:43.050 --> 00:52:45.570
The lazy strategy tries
to go a bit further,

00:52:45.570 --> 00:52:47.063
see if it can find
a bigger match.

00:52:47.063 --> 00:52:48.480
And if it finds a
bigger match, it

00:52:48.480 --> 00:52:52.110
takes the bigger match instead
of taking the greedy match.

00:52:52.110 --> 00:52:54.600
And you see that the
lazy strategy like always

00:52:54.600 --> 00:52:57.600
outperforms the greedy
strategy in this case

00:52:57.600 --> 00:52:59.040
for any minimum match.

00:53:04.770 --> 00:53:06.720
So yeah, I encourage
you to look at the code.

00:53:06.720 --> 00:53:09.708
I think only once you
look at it and you

00:53:09.708 --> 00:53:11.250
think about it a
little bit, you will

00:53:11.250 --> 00:53:12.780
appreciate this more and more.

00:53:12.780 --> 00:53:15.330
But the basic point
I'm trying to make

00:53:15.330 --> 00:53:19.440
is, lots of choices you can make
at the encoder, which lead to--

00:53:19.440 --> 00:53:22.500
maybe it makes it slower
but better compression.

00:53:22.500 --> 00:53:24.900
Or it takes more memory,
but better compression.

00:53:24.900 --> 00:53:28.470
So there are all
these trade-offs.

00:53:28.470 --> 00:53:32.550
Any questions so far
on this match finding?

00:53:32.550 --> 00:53:36.090
So one thing we try to do
in the implementation in SCL

00:53:36.090 --> 00:53:37.140
is we try to--

00:53:37.140 --> 00:53:40.200
not like this here, try to
separate the match finder

00:53:40.200 --> 00:53:41.700
so that you can plug and play.

00:53:41.700 --> 00:53:45.120
You can try different match
finders with the same encoder.

00:53:45.120 --> 00:53:47.100
The decoder doesn't
change, just the encoder,

00:53:47.100 --> 00:53:49.883
you can implement your
own favorite match finder.

00:53:49.883 --> 00:53:52.050
You can implement some DP,
dynamic programming-based

00:53:52.050 --> 00:53:52.550
thing.

00:53:56.160 --> 00:53:58.020
OK?

00:53:58.020 --> 00:54:00.750
And then, obviously, you need to
entropy code everything, right?

00:54:00.750 --> 00:54:02.510
It's not enough to--

00:54:02.510 --> 00:54:04.260
just producing, this
is not enough, right?

00:54:04.260 --> 00:54:07.380
At the end of the day, you need
to actually store it in bits.

00:54:07.380 --> 00:54:10.620
And that's, again, something
people have played with a lot.

00:54:10.620 --> 00:54:12.540
The current sort
of standard thing

00:54:12.540 --> 00:54:14.910
that everybody does, this
is not what gzip does,

00:54:14.910 --> 00:54:17.760
gzip does it a bit differently,
but all the modern compressors

00:54:17.760 --> 00:54:24.270
do this, is you take all the
unmatched literals in a block,

00:54:24.270 --> 00:54:29.900
you concatenate them together
into a single, long thing,

00:54:29.900 --> 00:54:31.550
and then you store
the number here--

00:54:31.550 --> 00:54:33.440
5, 0, 4, the length here.

00:54:33.440 --> 00:54:36.710
So, really, what you get-- this
is sort of a technical point,

00:54:36.710 --> 00:54:38.750
but really what you
have is you have

00:54:38.750 --> 00:54:41.720
the literals which is a
sequence of just, like, bytes.

00:54:41.720 --> 00:54:45.740
And you have three integer
sequences, literal counts,

00:54:45.740 --> 00:54:48.253
match length, match offset.

00:54:48.253 --> 00:54:50.420
Very technical point, don't
worry about it too much.

00:54:50.420 --> 00:54:53.540
But this is how everybody
does it nowadays.

00:54:53.540 --> 00:54:55.670
And then you have to
encode each of these.

00:54:55.670 --> 00:54:58.850
And we have all of the
entropy coders we saw, right?

00:54:58.850 --> 00:55:01.490
So that's the power of
these entropy coders

00:55:01.490 --> 00:55:04.190
because all LZ77 is
doing is converting

00:55:04.190 --> 00:55:07.160
your very complex stationary
distribution into a much

00:55:07.160 --> 00:55:09.770
simpler iid distribution.

00:55:09.770 --> 00:55:13.910
So now you can use your favorite
coders for iid distribution

00:55:13.910 --> 00:55:16.010
and they will basically
give you the results.

00:55:18.990 --> 00:55:19.490
Right?

00:55:19.490 --> 00:55:22.840
So you can use Huffman coding,
gzip just uses Huffman coding.

00:55:22.840 --> 00:55:24.950
Zstandard uses
Huffman for literals.

00:55:24.950 --> 00:55:29.210
You can use tANS, which is
recently becoming more popular.

00:55:29.210 --> 00:55:31.690
C-standard uses it for--

00:55:31.690 --> 00:55:33.790
oh, what are this?

00:55:33.790 --> 00:55:36.560
Not for literals.

00:55:36.560 --> 00:55:40.210
It uses for match
lengths and offsets.

00:55:40.210 --> 00:55:42.400
Yeah.

00:55:42.400 --> 00:55:45.400
Or you can use context-based
arithmetic coding,

00:55:45.400 --> 00:55:47.920
which is very slow to
encode and decode, which

00:55:47.920 --> 00:55:52.987
is what LZMA or XZ or
7-Zip, all these guys use.

00:55:52.987 --> 00:55:54.570
Or you could just
skip entropy coding.

00:55:54.570 --> 00:55:55.560
Say that, OK, I don't care.

00:55:55.560 --> 00:55:56.850
I will just use a simple code.

00:55:56.850 --> 00:55:59.640
I won't do like any fancy
entropy coding because I

00:55:59.640 --> 00:56:00.780
want to be very, very fast.

00:56:00.780 --> 00:56:04.050
And that's what
LZ4 and Snappy do.

00:56:04.050 --> 00:56:09.060
All these names probably don't
sound very familiar to you.

00:56:09.060 --> 00:56:09.882
And that's fine.

00:56:09.882 --> 00:56:12.090
I think really there are
only a couple of compressors

00:56:12.090 --> 00:56:14.382
you should know about, really,
and then everything else

00:56:14.382 --> 00:56:16.440
you can--

00:56:16.440 --> 00:56:19.780
if you need it, you
look at it, sort of.

00:56:19.780 --> 00:56:20.920
Let me skip this slide.

00:56:20.920 --> 00:56:23.410
It's like a technical point
about how do you actually

00:56:23.410 --> 00:56:25.840
entropy code integers.

00:56:25.840 --> 00:56:28.860
Please read it at home.

00:56:28.860 --> 00:56:32.400
OK, so, really, the lesson
is that the parsing or match

00:56:32.400 --> 00:56:35.040
finding strategy, the window
size, the entropy coder,

00:56:35.040 --> 00:56:36.553
implementation
details, like, are

00:56:36.553 --> 00:56:38.970
you using SIMD, are you using
these advanced instructions,

00:56:38.970 --> 00:56:42.300
are you parallelizing things
matter a lot in determining

00:56:42.300 --> 00:56:43.890
speed and memory usage.

00:56:43.890 --> 00:56:46.800
So let's look at this plot.

00:56:46.800 --> 00:56:48.780
So this is showing
Zstandard, which

00:56:48.780 --> 00:56:53.490
is a modern LZ77-based
compressor and Zlib, which

00:56:53.490 --> 00:56:55.530
is the library
behind gzip, which

00:56:55.530 --> 00:57:00.270
is an older, around like 30
years old now, compressor.

00:57:00.270 --> 00:57:03.630
The x-axis is compression speed.

00:57:03.630 --> 00:57:06.360
The y-axis is compression ratio.

00:57:06.360 --> 00:57:09.300
And where you want to be
is on this side, basically.

00:57:09.300 --> 00:57:12.420
You want higher speed, lower
compression ratio-- sorry,

00:57:12.420 --> 00:57:15.640
higher speed and higher
compression ratio.

00:57:15.640 --> 00:57:17.620
And you see-- and the
different dots here

00:57:17.620 --> 00:57:20.530
are the different levels, like,
different parameters where

00:57:20.530 --> 00:57:23.320
you take a bigger window, or
you use a more powerful entropy

00:57:23.320 --> 00:57:24.340
coder.

00:57:24.340 --> 00:57:27.710
Sorry, not-- use a more
powerful match finder.

00:57:27.710 --> 00:57:33.050
And you see that zstandard just
like beats Zlib on all fronts.

00:57:33.050 --> 00:57:34.910
Partly because it has
better algorithms,

00:57:34.910 --> 00:57:36.800
partly because it
uses more memory,

00:57:36.800 --> 00:57:40.100
partly because it is implemented
better to use modern hardware

00:57:40.100 --> 00:57:41.120
instructions.

00:57:41.120 --> 00:57:44.720
So all of these things
matter, these small decisions

00:57:44.720 --> 00:57:48.860
end up impacting where you
lie in this curve, basically.

00:57:51.390 --> 00:57:55.320
And this is decompression
speed where, again, zstandard

00:57:55.320 --> 00:57:56.940
achieves like amazing results.

00:57:56.940 --> 00:57:59.940
It's like 1.4 GBPS in this--

00:57:59.940 --> 00:58:02.130
for some data set.

00:58:02.130 --> 00:58:06.390
Again, this is mostly because of
how the decoder is implemented.

00:58:06.390 --> 00:58:07.440
That's a big role.

00:58:07.440 --> 00:58:09.960
And also because how they
structured the format, which

00:58:09.960 --> 00:58:11.670
is a bit technical.

00:58:11.670 --> 00:58:13.500
LZMA uses arithmetic coding.

00:58:13.500 --> 00:58:15.870
So it's just very
slow to decode, sadly.

00:58:15.870 --> 00:58:20.300
Arithmetic decoding, as
we saw, is slow to decode.

00:58:20.300 --> 00:58:23.180
Another thing you notice is that
we don't show any levels here.

00:58:23.180 --> 00:58:25.770
And the reason is
that regardless

00:58:25.770 --> 00:58:29.130
of what match finding you use,
the decoding stays the same.

00:58:29.130 --> 00:58:31.650
Decoding is just copying
stuff from the past

00:58:31.650 --> 00:58:35.910
so it affects us slightly,
but not too much.

00:58:35.910 --> 00:58:39.240
The different encoders can
still produce the same sort

00:58:39.240 --> 00:58:41.280
of decompression speed.

00:58:46.560 --> 00:58:48.330
And then, here, you
can just like look

00:58:48.330 --> 00:58:51.300
at so many different
LZ77, each of them

00:58:51.300 --> 00:58:53.490
have their own levels
from one to whatever.

00:58:53.490 --> 00:58:55.470
You have the zstandard,
zlib is gzip,

00:58:55.470 --> 00:58:57.990
basically, you have
Brotli from Google.

00:58:57.990 --> 00:59:00.450
You have LZ4, again,
from Yann Collet

00:59:00.450 --> 00:59:02.130
who also wrote zstandard.

00:59:02.130 --> 00:59:03.840
You have Snappy from Google.

00:59:03.840 --> 00:59:07.710
So, just all these guys keep
making their own versions.

00:59:07.710 --> 00:59:10.243
And we'll look at for,
like a practitioner,

00:59:10.243 --> 00:59:11.410
how do you think about this?

00:59:11.410 --> 00:59:12.660
There are so many compressors.

00:59:12.660 --> 00:59:14.250
Like, you Google
data compression.

00:59:14.250 --> 00:59:16.170
You get like a million results.

00:59:16.170 --> 00:59:17.910
What do you do?

00:59:17.910 --> 00:59:20.455
Before we go there, just a
quick note that universal

00:59:20.455 --> 00:59:21.330
doesn't mean perfect.

00:59:21.330 --> 00:59:22.760
Right?

00:59:22.760 --> 00:59:25.790
LZ77 is universal in
an asymptotic sense.

00:59:25.790 --> 00:59:27.530
Again, as n goes to
infinity, it will

00:59:27.530 --> 00:59:30.560
achieve the entropy wrote for
any stationary distribution.

00:59:30.560 --> 00:59:33.350
But your data is probably
not infinitely large.

00:59:33.350 --> 00:59:34.760
It's a finite thing.

00:59:34.760 --> 00:59:37.370
So it need not be the best
choice for your particular data

00:59:37.370 --> 00:59:38.810
set.

00:59:38.810 --> 00:59:42.200
Even if it is the perfect choice
in terms of compression ratio,

00:59:42.200 --> 00:59:43.700
maybe it is too slow for you.

00:59:43.700 --> 00:59:46.633
Maybe it has too high
of a memory usage.

00:59:46.633 --> 00:59:48.800
So all these things should
go into your calculation.

00:59:48.800 --> 00:59:51.497
Just don't think that
LZ77 is universal,

00:59:51.497 --> 00:59:52.580
so that's what I will use.

00:59:52.580 --> 00:59:54.440
I will never consider
any other compressor.

00:59:54.440 --> 00:59:57.680
That's not how we should think.

00:59:57.680 --> 01:00:01.250
And obviously, as we
saw, right, gzip is LZ77,

01:00:01.250 --> 01:00:04.190
zstandard is LZ77, but
they are not the same.

01:00:04.190 --> 01:00:07.760
So there are a lot of, like,
beyond this base algorithm,

01:00:07.760 --> 01:00:10.490
the basic idea that works
in theory, there are so many

01:00:10.490 --> 01:00:12.290
minor things you can do which--

01:00:12.290 --> 01:00:14.870
or major things you can do,
which really change the world.

01:00:17.180 --> 01:00:17.680
OK.

01:00:17.680 --> 01:00:19.810
So that's it on LZ.

01:00:19.810 --> 01:00:21.430
We didn't talk about LZ78.

01:00:21.430 --> 01:00:22.480
It is very interesting.

01:00:22.480 --> 01:00:25.270
I think-- I encourage
you to read up on it,

01:00:25.270 --> 01:00:29.170
or do a project on
it, if you want to.

01:00:29.170 --> 01:00:31.810
Tsachy covers it in
much detail in EE 376C.

01:00:31.810 --> 01:00:37.670
The course is called "Universal
Schemes In Information Theory."

01:00:37.670 --> 01:00:40.250
It's a bit like
theoretical course.

01:00:40.250 --> 01:00:44.027
I think quite a while
since it was last taught,

01:00:44.027 --> 01:00:45.860
but I think we can share
the notes with you,

01:00:45.860 --> 01:00:49.370
if you are interested in
the theory aspect here.

01:00:49.370 --> 01:00:52.070
And there are a lot
of things in LZ77--

01:00:52.070 --> 01:00:54.080
things like repcodes,
optimal parsing,

01:00:54.080 --> 01:00:56.090
which we don't have
time to cover here.

01:00:56.090 --> 01:00:58.610
All of these are good
project ideas, talk to us

01:00:58.610 --> 01:01:03.710
and we can point you to
the relevant resources.

01:01:03.710 --> 01:01:04.280
OK.

01:01:04.280 --> 01:01:11.420
So let's-- so short, the
summary is that LZ77 is a great

01:01:11.420 --> 01:01:12.230
compressor.

01:01:12.230 --> 01:01:13.798
So now-- but you
have been-- like,

01:01:13.798 --> 01:01:16.340
every compressor we teach you,
we tell you it's a great idea.

01:01:16.340 --> 01:01:18.890
Last time, we said context-based
arithmetic coding is great.

01:01:18.890 --> 01:01:21.680
Before that, Huffman was great.

01:01:21.680 --> 01:01:24.500
Everybody seems
optimal in some sense.

01:01:24.500 --> 01:01:27.290
So how do you think
about data compression?

01:01:27.290 --> 01:01:29.620
OK.

01:01:29.620 --> 01:01:31.268
So, you have a lot of data.

01:01:31.268 --> 01:01:32.560
You want to compress that data.

01:01:32.560 --> 01:01:33.190
First thing.

01:01:33.190 --> 01:01:34.870
First thing, always
to think about,

01:01:34.870 --> 01:01:37.240
is it possible you don't
need all this data?

01:01:37.240 --> 01:01:38.530
Do you really need this data?

01:01:38.530 --> 01:01:40.900
Maybe just delete it.

01:01:40.900 --> 01:01:42.220
Really, this is very important.

01:01:42.220 --> 01:01:46.450
And like, that's how
most of the cost savings

01:01:46.450 --> 01:01:49.390
come from, because you just
identify that, oh, I don't

01:01:49.390 --> 01:01:51.010
need this exabyte of data.

01:01:51.010 --> 01:01:52.450
Let's just delete the data.

01:01:52.450 --> 01:01:56.710
And that's basically saving
you millions of dollars.

01:01:56.710 --> 01:02:00.510
So again, maybe you can't
delete the whole thing,

01:02:00.510 --> 01:02:02.550
but maybe you can
identify with whatever

01:02:02.550 --> 01:02:04.860
you have learned that
some parts of the data

01:02:04.860 --> 01:02:07.080
are costing you more than
other parts of the data.

01:02:07.080 --> 01:02:10.390
Let's say, you
are writing a log.

01:02:10.390 --> 01:02:12.490
There are multiple fields
in the log, let's say.

01:02:12.490 --> 01:02:15.520
Maybe some fields are
much more compressible.

01:02:15.520 --> 01:02:17.530
Things that look very
long, like if you

01:02:17.530 --> 01:02:20.410
have a log of some
sort of web server log,

01:02:20.410 --> 01:02:23.590
for example, it will
have the host name.

01:02:23.590 --> 01:02:26.132
But maybe the host name is
the same for every log line

01:02:26.132 --> 01:02:28.090
because the host name is
the name of that host.

01:02:28.090 --> 01:02:29.710
It is the IP address
of that host.

01:02:29.710 --> 01:02:31.395
So anything that
just keeps repeating

01:02:31.395 --> 01:02:32.770
is going to be
very compressible,

01:02:32.770 --> 01:02:34.450
even if it looks long.

01:02:34.450 --> 01:02:37.450
But maybe there is some
request ID or some access ID

01:02:37.450 --> 01:02:40.060
or something, which is
like a random small string.

01:02:40.060 --> 01:02:42.220
It looks very small
when you look at it

01:02:42.220 --> 01:02:43.750
in the uncompressed domain.

01:02:43.750 --> 01:02:46.230
If you have like--

01:02:46.230 --> 01:02:49.520
if you have hostname,
which is like 10.1--

01:02:49.520 --> 01:02:54.860
sorry, 10.1.1.12 or
something like this,

01:02:54.860 --> 01:02:57.515
and then that just
repeats every line,

01:02:57.515 --> 01:02:59.390
so this is-- like, even
though it looks long,

01:02:59.390 --> 01:03:00.800
it's easy to compress.

01:03:00.800 --> 01:03:03.350
It doesn't take any bits
in the compressed domain.

01:03:03.350 --> 01:03:05.390
But maybe you have this
like random thing--

01:03:05.390 --> 01:03:11.880
XYA, ZXX, BDC,
something like this.

01:03:11.880 --> 01:03:12.570
Right?

01:03:12.570 --> 01:03:15.198
This is very hard to compress.

01:03:15.198 --> 01:03:17.490
So that sort of thing, like,
after you do a compression

01:03:17.490 --> 01:03:19.032
class, you should
have some intuition

01:03:19.032 --> 01:03:22.560
like, does my identifier, my
uuid, some unique identifier

01:03:22.560 --> 01:03:24.000
does it need to be so random?

01:03:24.000 --> 01:03:25.620
Does it need to be 64 bytes?

01:03:25.620 --> 01:03:27.690
Can I do it with like 10 bytes?

01:03:27.690 --> 01:03:30.300
Can I make it sequential,
instead of random?

01:03:30.300 --> 01:03:33.220
So just, like, this is how
you can think about it.

01:03:33.220 --> 01:03:33.720
Right?

01:03:33.720 --> 01:03:36.540
For any type of data,
just to plug an example.

01:03:39.212 --> 01:03:40.670
If you're storing
this data, right?

01:03:40.670 --> 01:03:42.860
And again, I'm thinking more
from like a company perspective

01:03:42.860 --> 01:03:45.260
rather than personal storage,
but even personal storage,

01:03:45.260 --> 01:03:48.500
you take these photos,
do you need all of them,

01:03:48.500 --> 01:03:49.760
to look at them, really?

01:03:49.760 --> 01:03:53.362
So for companies,
obviously, sometimes there

01:03:53.362 --> 01:03:54.320
are legal requirements.

01:03:54.320 --> 01:03:56.120
You need to keep some
data for some time,

01:03:56.120 --> 01:03:58.160
or maybe there are reasons
you want to keep it.

01:03:58.160 --> 01:04:00.077
So sure, OK, maybe, you
need to keep the data.

01:04:00.077 --> 01:04:02.250
But think hard about this.

01:04:02.250 --> 01:04:06.270
And finally, think about what
is your data access pattern?

01:04:06.270 --> 01:04:08.590
Take photos, for example.

01:04:08.590 --> 01:04:10.170
Photos maybe you don't--

01:04:10.170 --> 01:04:12.960
you want to keep them, maybe
in future, at some point,

01:04:12.960 --> 01:04:14.070
you want to look at them.

01:04:14.070 --> 01:04:16.650
But it's not like you're going
through your like one-year-old

01:04:16.650 --> 01:04:17.910
photos every day.

01:04:17.910 --> 01:04:20.310
So maybe, you store it in
a cheaper, cold storage

01:04:20.310 --> 01:04:22.020
sort of thing, rather
than storing it

01:04:22.020 --> 01:04:24.480
in the most expensive storage.

01:04:24.480 --> 01:04:25.350
Right?

01:04:25.350 --> 01:04:29.840
So just like a computer,
if you read about cache

01:04:29.840 --> 01:04:30.840
in your computer, right?

01:04:30.840 --> 01:04:33.420
It keeps the things that
are more frequently accessed

01:04:33.420 --> 01:04:35.910
in a very sort of
expensive storage.

01:04:35.910 --> 01:04:38.160
And it keeps everything
that's used less in like RAM

01:04:38.160 --> 01:04:39.870
or on disk, or something.

01:04:39.870 --> 01:04:42.480
Similarly, store your
data in that way.

01:04:42.480 --> 01:04:45.750
And finally, for a
lot of types of data,

01:04:45.750 --> 01:04:47.340
you can't do
lossless compression

01:04:47.340 --> 01:04:49.110
and be, like, practical.

01:04:49.110 --> 01:04:51.570
Videos are the prime
example of that.

01:04:51.570 --> 01:04:53.760
And that's basically the
next half of the course

01:04:53.760 --> 01:04:55.822
where sometimes just
lossy compression,

01:04:55.822 --> 01:04:57.780
it's just unreasonable
to do lossy compression.

01:04:57.780 --> 01:05:00.770
So, OK.

01:05:00.770 --> 01:05:03.285
And one more thing, before
you get too discouraged,

01:05:03.285 --> 01:05:04.910
why do we learn all
this if you're just

01:05:04.910 --> 01:05:06.380
going to delete all our data.

01:05:06.380 --> 01:05:08.070
Compression is not
only about storage.

01:05:08.070 --> 01:05:10.280
It reduces memory,
it reduces bandwidth.

01:05:10.280 --> 01:05:13.040
You can sometimes query your
data faster with compression.

01:05:13.040 --> 01:05:15.890
So many times, you
need to do compression

01:05:15.890 --> 01:05:18.980
and you need to know
how to do compression.

01:05:18.980 --> 01:05:22.560
OK, any questions?

01:05:22.560 --> 01:05:23.160
Yeah.

01:05:23.160 --> 01:05:24.840
Some of this might
sound like a joke,

01:05:24.840 --> 01:05:26.257
but, like, when
we talk to people,

01:05:26.257 --> 01:05:27.990
and this is what
the unique thing

01:05:27.990 --> 01:05:30.540
you can offer once you
know about compression.

01:05:30.540 --> 01:05:32.820
Many of these things are not
at all obvious to people.

01:05:32.820 --> 01:05:34.800
Like, this thing
where people think

01:05:34.800 --> 01:05:36.420
that, oh, this is
so long, this should

01:05:36.420 --> 01:05:37.800
be very hard to compress.

01:05:37.800 --> 01:05:38.370
Right?

01:05:38.370 --> 01:05:40.050
But now, you know
that the entropy here

01:05:40.050 --> 01:05:42.820
is zero, basically, because
everything is the same.

01:05:42.820 --> 01:05:43.320
Right?

01:05:43.320 --> 01:05:45.945
You can easily predict the next
one, based on the previous one.

01:05:45.945 --> 01:05:48.000
Where here, you have
a lot of entropy,

01:05:48.000 --> 01:05:49.800
predicting the next one is
not-- because they are random,

01:05:49.800 --> 01:05:50.280
actually.

01:05:50.280 --> 01:05:50.700
Right?

01:05:50.700 --> 01:05:52.140
So this is sort
of intuition that

01:05:52.140 --> 01:05:56.120
will be useful, like, when you
look at a real application.

01:05:56.120 --> 01:05:57.830
OK, so now you
decide, OK, I need

01:05:57.830 --> 01:05:59.240
to store this data losslessly.

01:05:59.240 --> 01:05:59.840
Now what?

01:05:59.840 --> 01:06:03.000
Now, what's the next step?

01:06:03.000 --> 01:06:05.090
The things not to do.

01:06:05.090 --> 01:06:07.750
OK, so maybe you think,
OK, I learned EE274.

01:06:07.750 --> 01:06:09.220
Now, I'm very smart.

01:06:09.220 --> 01:06:12.880
I will just implement
my own compressor.

01:06:12.880 --> 01:06:13.540
Right?

01:06:13.540 --> 01:06:17.470
I will use LZ77 and
I will use Huffman,

01:06:17.470 --> 01:06:22.090
or maybe I will just design
my own LLM or something.

01:06:22.090 --> 01:06:24.370
OK, don't do this.

01:06:24.370 --> 01:06:26.290
I will talk about why.

01:06:26.290 --> 01:06:28.960
Or maybe you think, OK,
I will not implement

01:06:28.960 --> 01:06:32.110
and I will not discover
a new algorithm

01:06:32.110 --> 01:06:35.440
or invent a new algorithm, but
I know all these LZ77 entropy

01:06:35.440 --> 01:06:36.040
coders.

01:06:36.040 --> 01:06:39.700
So let me implement them, right?

01:06:39.700 --> 01:06:41.800
Let me implement them
from scratch in Python

01:06:41.800 --> 01:06:46.330
or in C. Or maybe you say,
no, I'm just not up to it.

01:06:46.330 --> 01:06:48.790
I didn't like listening the
lectures carefully enough.

01:06:48.790 --> 01:06:51.910
I will just Google
search online and see

01:06:51.910 --> 01:06:57.500
some very expensive compressor,
and just buy the license to it.

01:06:57.500 --> 01:07:00.650
Or, maybe you just, like,
listen to some small part

01:07:00.650 --> 01:07:04.010
of this lecture, you
decide, LZ77 is universal,

01:07:04.010 --> 01:07:06.140
gzip is an
implementation of LZ77.

01:07:06.140 --> 01:07:07.980
It has to be the
perfect compressor.

01:07:07.980 --> 01:07:08.480
Right?

01:07:08.480 --> 01:07:10.940
You didn't listen to the
lecture very carefully.

01:07:10.940 --> 01:07:13.820
And-- OK, that's the
popular thing, right?

01:07:13.820 --> 01:07:16.020
Everybody says gzip is
the compressor to use.

01:07:16.020 --> 01:07:16.520
OK.

01:07:16.520 --> 01:07:17.020
Sure.

01:07:17.020 --> 01:07:17.810
We'll use that.

01:07:17.810 --> 01:07:19.830
So don't do this.

01:07:19.830 --> 01:07:22.670
Use your skills from this class.

01:07:22.670 --> 01:07:25.700
All of this can make sense
but not as the first step.

01:07:25.700 --> 01:07:29.710
The first thing you should do is
to understand your application.

01:07:29.710 --> 01:07:31.090
Why are you using compression?

01:07:31.090 --> 01:07:32.530
What is the need?

01:07:32.530 --> 01:07:34.420
And where are you
using compression?

01:07:34.420 --> 01:07:36.070
So what is the speed you need?

01:07:36.070 --> 01:07:37.930
What is the memory
usage you need?

01:07:37.930 --> 01:07:39.640
Where is the
compression happening?

01:07:39.640 --> 01:07:41.230
Is it happening on
your own machine?

01:07:41.230 --> 01:07:43.270
Is it happening on
somebody else's machine?

01:07:43.270 --> 01:07:44.770
Is it an open source library?

01:07:44.770 --> 01:07:48.670
Is it a closed source thing
within your organization?

01:07:48.670 --> 01:07:49.930
Is it like a hobby project?

01:07:49.930 --> 01:07:51.472
Is it like something
that needs to be

01:07:51.472 --> 01:07:57.520
used in production on, like,
gigabytes or petabytes of data?

01:07:57.520 --> 01:07:59.500
How will compression and
decompression happen?

01:07:59.500 --> 01:08:01.125
We don't talk about
it as often, right,

01:08:01.125 --> 01:08:03.910
but gzip, zstandard,
all of these have a CLI,

01:08:03.910 --> 01:08:05.620
they have a command
line interface.

01:08:05.620 --> 01:08:10.370
You can run it like zstandard,
dash c, whatever, whatever.

01:08:10.370 --> 01:08:13.580
Or they have a library, there
is a C library, Python library,

01:08:13.580 --> 01:08:16.520
Java library, all
languages imaginable,

01:08:16.520 --> 01:08:18.770
there is a zstandard
or a gzip library.

01:08:18.770 --> 01:08:21.250
So where will it happen?

01:08:21.250 --> 01:08:23.590
Is the data homogeneous?

01:08:23.590 --> 01:08:27.880
So for example, do you have
a lot of genomics data?

01:08:27.880 --> 01:08:30.370
So all of the data looks
similar to each other.

01:08:30.370 --> 01:08:32.740
Or are you like a
cloud storage service,

01:08:32.740 --> 01:08:35.649
where everybody wants to store
their data on your system?

01:08:35.649 --> 01:08:37.569
So therefore the data
is not up to you.

01:08:37.569 --> 01:08:40.180
Everybody else decides what
data they put in your thing.

01:08:40.180 --> 01:08:42.640
So whether the data
is homogeneous or not

01:08:42.640 --> 01:08:44.779
will influence how you
design your compressor.

01:08:44.779 --> 01:08:45.279
Right?

01:08:45.279 --> 01:08:46.689
If it's homogeneous,
if you have a lot

01:08:46.689 --> 01:08:49.240
of the data of the same kind,
you have lots of genomics data,

01:08:49.240 --> 01:08:51.430
maybe you want to design
your own special compressor

01:08:51.430 --> 01:08:52.750
for genomics.

01:08:52.750 --> 01:08:54.910
But if it's not homogeneous,
so other people are

01:08:54.910 --> 01:08:56.620
putting in their
data, you don't know

01:08:56.620 --> 01:08:57.828
what the data is going to be.

01:08:57.828 --> 01:09:00.439
So then maybe you should use a
universal compressor like LZ.

01:09:00.439 --> 01:09:00.939
Right?

01:09:00.939 --> 01:09:02.500
So all of this
should influence how

01:09:02.500 --> 01:09:04.899
you think about
building your own thing,

01:09:04.899 --> 01:09:06.385
or not building your own thing.

01:09:09.180 --> 01:09:11.850
So one thing I find
often useful is

01:09:11.850 --> 01:09:14.580
to use these benchmarks
that people have done,

01:09:14.580 --> 01:09:16.680
or you can do.

01:09:16.680 --> 01:09:20.550
The idea is that you take some
corpus, you take some data set,

01:09:20.550 --> 01:09:22.109
like maybe you
take genomics data,

01:09:22.109 --> 01:09:24.600
maybe you take a mixture of
different types of files.

01:09:24.600 --> 01:09:26.670
You run a bunch of
different compressors

01:09:26.670 --> 01:09:31.620
in their different parameters,
and then you make a plot.

01:09:31.620 --> 01:09:35.390
So you make a plot,
like this one.

01:09:35.390 --> 01:09:39.890
So the x-axis is
compression speed.

01:09:39.890 --> 01:09:43.510
So this is better, basically.

01:09:43.510 --> 01:09:47.920
Let me just write
faster compression.

01:09:47.920 --> 01:09:49.899
And this is more compression.

01:09:53.930 --> 01:09:57.110
And you see, for example,
that if speed is your thing,

01:09:57.110 --> 01:09:58.340
LZ4 is the way to go.

01:09:58.340 --> 01:10:00.230
There is nobody
even close to it.

01:10:00.230 --> 01:10:04.490
It's just like several times
faster than everybody else.

01:10:04.490 --> 01:10:06.240
Maybe if you want the
extreme compression,

01:10:06.240 --> 01:10:08.460
then these things
called ZPAQ or ZMIX,

01:10:08.460 --> 01:10:11.130
or these very, very
slow but very powerful

01:10:11.130 --> 01:10:13.590
compressors, maybe
those are the way to go.

01:10:13.590 --> 01:10:15.090
Maybe we want
something in between.

01:10:15.090 --> 01:10:17.490
So maybe you want
to use zstandard.

01:10:17.490 --> 01:10:19.170
Maybe you want something
slightly slower

01:10:19.170 --> 01:10:21.212
than zstandard that gives
you better compression.

01:10:21.212 --> 01:10:23.282
So maybe you use BZIP2 or BSC.

01:10:23.282 --> 01:10:24.990
These might just sound
like names to you,

01:10:24.990 --> 01:10:26.240
if you have not heard of them.

01:10:26.240 --> 01:10:28.260
But basically these are
the popular compressors

01:10:28.260 --> 01:10:31.490
that many times people
would want to consider.

01:10:31.490 --> 01:10:35.440
In this plot, this
green one is gzip,

01:10:35.440 --> 01:10:37.870
this brown one is zstandard.

01:10:37.870 --> 01:10:40.060
Would you ever use
gzip over zstandard

01:10:40.060 --> 01:10:41.095
by looking at this plot?

01:10:46.390 --> 01:10:48.390
At the top right is the
better direction, right?

01:10:48.390 --> 01:10:50.850
And you see just the zstandard,
every level of zstandard

01:10:50.850 --> 01:10:52.860
is just better than--

01:10:52.860 --> 01:10:55.350
zstandard just gives you a
better Pareto optimal sort

01:10:55.350 --> 01:10:57.525
of frontier, as
compared to gzip,

01:10:57.525 --> 01:11:01.020
because it's very similar
idea but just modern.

01:11:01.020 --> 01:11:02.970
It does use more
memory, so if you

01:11:02.970 --> 01:11:05.280
are like running
on a Raspberry Pi,

01:11:05.280 --> 01:11:08.940
or you're running on like a
1970s computer for some reason,

01:11:08.940 --> 01:11:10.800
then you want to use
gzip because gzip

01:11:10.800 --> 01:11:13.680
can work with even kilobytes
of memory, versus zstandard,

01:11:13.680 --> 01:11:15.353
would at least take megabytes.

01:11:15.353 --> 01:11:16.770
When I was small,
I had a computer

01:11:16.770 --> 01:11:18.930
just like 128 MB total RAM.

01:11:18.930 --> 01:11:21.060
So there, I could
imagine like zstandard

01:11:21.060 --> 01:11:22.650
would might be too expensive.

01:11:22.650 --> 01:11:27.090
But really, today,
it's not that common.

01:11:27.090 --> 01:11:29.500
Same for decompression speed.

01:11:29.500 --> 01:11:35.680
So this is, like,
faster decompression.

01:11:35.680 --> 01:11:38.530
And this is more
compression, basically.

01:11:38.530 --> 01:11:42.010
So again, the
y-axis is the same.

01:11:42.010 --> 01:11:45.030
And here you again see
LZ4 is the clear winner.

01:11:45.030 --> 01:11:47.597
Fast compression or fast
decompression, just use LZ4.

01:11:47.597 --> 01:11:48.930
Don't worry about anything else.

01:11:48.930 --> 01:11:50.495
All of them have
multiple parameters.

01:11:50.495 --> 01:11:52.620
So that's why you see
multiple points corresponding

01:11:52.620 --> 01:11:54.530
to the same thing.

01:11:54.530 --> 01:11:56.690
Zstandard is very fast,
Brotli is very fast,

01:11:56.690 --> 01:11:59.060
gzip is slower, but
still pretty fast.

01:11:59.060 --> 01:12:03.560
And the reason is all of
these are based on LZ77.

01:12:03.560 --> 01:12:05.860
And LZ77--

01:12:05.860 --> 01:12:09.850
So what about the memory
usage for gzip and zstandard?

01:12:09.850 --> 01:12:10.450
Yeah.

01:12:10.450 --> 01:12:13.960
So zstandard uses more memory
because it-- by default,

01:12:13.960 --> 01:12:16.210
uses a bigger window than gzip.

01:12:16.210 --> 01:12:17.950
Gzip, the maximum
window is, like,

01:12:17.950 --> 01:12:21.310
I think 32 kilobytes or 64
kilobytes, something like that.

01:12:21.310 --> 01:12:24.670
Zstandard you can increase it to
even gigabytes if you want to.

01:12:24.670 --> 01:12:27.010
But by default, it's
like a few megabytes.

01:12:27.010 --> 01:12:30.280
So the memory usage of zstandard
is like in tens of megabytes,

01:12:30.280 --> 01:12:34.270
typically, whereas gzip is like
a couple megabytes or less.

01:12:34.270 --> 01:12:36.490
So that's the rough thing.

01:12:36.490 --> 01:12:38.170
Most applications
today, I think,

01:12:38.170 --> 01:12:39.910
are fine with the
zstandard memory usage.

01:12:39.910 --> 01:12:41.800
But if you're in a
very constrained host,

01:12:41.800 --> 01:12:45.430
or constrained system, then
you might not be able to do it.

01:12:45.430 --> 01:12:46.030
Yeah.

01:12:46.030 --> 01:12:47.410
So that's like a
trade-off they made.

01:12:47.410 --> 01:12:48.640
Like, it's not
like the zstandard

01:12:48.640 --> 01:12:49.960
is just better on every front.

01:12:49.960 --> 01:12:52.543
There is still, like, the memory
front where it's not so good.

01:12:52.543 --> 01:12:54.820
But I think it's just
made for a modern age.

01:12:54.820 --> 01:12:56.500
Where gzip made some decisions.

01:12:56.500 --> 01:12:59.290
For example, they fixed
their maximum window size

01:12:59.290 --> 01:13:01.930
to be this 32 KB, or 64.

01:13:01.930 --> 01:13:02.950
I don't remember.

01:13:02.950 --> 01:13:06.070
There is no way in the format
to allow it to increase.

01:13:06.070 --> 01:13:08.440
Zstandard learned
from that lesson.

01:13:08.440 --> 01:13:10.510
They don't put a upper
limit on the window size.

01:13:10.510 --> 01:13:12.052
They say, maybe, in
future there will

01:13:12.052 --> 01:13:15.520
be a machine which will be able
to handle a 1 gigabyte or a 1

01:13:15.520 --> 01:13:17.380
terabyte window.

01:13:17.380 --> 01:13:19.242
Why not let it use that?

01:13:19.242 --> 01:13:21.700
We'll just-- so there are these
decisions that people make.

01:13:21.700 --> 01:13:25.530
So I think zstandard was
much more careful in, like,

01:13:25.530 --> 01:13:30.030
making these decisions on what
the format is in that sense.

01:13:30.030 --> 01:13:31.950
You can use zstandard
with a very low memory,

01:13:31.950 --> 01:13:34.408
if you use a very small window.

01:13:34.408 --> 01:13:36.730
Yeah.

01:13:36.730 --> 01:13:39.310
And then, if you want
more compression,

01:13:39.310 --> 01:13:41.110
but you're fine with
slower decompression,

01:13:41.110 --> 01:13:44.992
use BSC or BZIP, which I
will talk about in a second.

01:13:44.992 --> 01:13:46.450
But yeah, so the
point I was making

01:13:46.450 --> 01:13:48.940
was that LZ77 is
fast at decompression

01:13:48.940 --> 01:13:52.410
because the decompression,
as you saw before,

01:13:52.410 --> 01:13:54.030
is just copying from the past.

01:13:54.030 --> 01:13:55.710
And mem copies are very fast.

01:13:55.710 --> 01:13:57.600
There isn't like a
lot of logic there.

01:13:57.600 --> 01:14:00.150
You do Huffman decoding, you
do ANS decoding, all of them

01:14:00.150 --> 01:14:00.840
are very fast.

01:14:00.840 --> 01:14:03.510
And then you just copy
stuff from the past.

01:14:03.510 --> 01:14:08.790
So general rule of thumb is
zstandard has a very fast

01:14:08.790 --> 01:14:11.120
decompression at any level.

01:14:11.120 --> 01:14:12.740
It has a lot of levels.

01:14:12.740 --> 01:14:16.340
It should be the first thing to
try, I think, in today's age.

01:14:16.340 --> 01:14:18.230
If you are given a
compression problem,

01:14:18.230 --> 01:14:21.002
try zstandard first,
and don't use gzip

01:14:21.002 --> 01:14:22.460
unless you have a
very good reason.

01:14:22.460 --> 01:14:24.345
Maybe you have some
compatibility reason.

01:14:24.345 --> 01:14:25.220
A lot of people have.

01:14:25.220 --> 01:14:27.950
If you are writing data that
other people need to read,

01:14:27.950 --> 01:14:31.028
and they don't have zstandard
on their system, then maybe you

01:14:31.028 --> 01:14:31.820
need to write gzip.

01:14:31.820 --> 01:14:36.340
But other than that
really, don't use gzip.

01:14:36.340 --> 01:14:38.020
To go even faster, try LZ4.

01:14:38.020 --> 01:14:39.970
So what LZ4 does
different from zstandard

01:14:39.970 --> 01:14:42.400
is that LZ4 has a very
fast match finder.

01:14:42.400 --> 01:14:44.290
And it doesn't do
any entropy coding.

01:14:44.290 --> 01:14:47.170
So it skips the ANS
or the Huffman step.

01:14:47.170 --> 01:14:49.960
It just encodes it with
some fixed number of bits.

01:14:49.960 --> 01:14:52.645
So it's much, much
faster in that way.

01:14:55.400 --> 01:14:57.440
Now, if you want to go
a bit slower, right,

01:14:57.440 --> 01:15:00.560
you think that, OK, I need to
store this data for 10 years,

01:15:00.560 --> 01:15:03.950
I don't work with it so often
it's more for an archival type

01:15:03.950 --> 01:15:04.670
use case.

01:15:04.670 --> 01:15:06.710
Maybe you want like
a better compressor.

01:15:06.710 --> 01:15:08.780
So now there are two
choices, mainly, one

01:15:08.780 --> 01:15:12.500
is this LZMA-based compressors
which are again LZ77 followed

01:15:12.500 --> 01:15:14.570
by arithmetic
coding, which we know

01:15:14.570 --> 01:15:17.780
can be more powerful
than like Huffman or ANS

01:15:17.780 --> 01:15:19.760
because you can do
context-based coding.

01:15:19.760 --> 01:15:22.040
And XZ, if you have
heard of it, or 7-zip

01:15:22.040 --> 01:15:24.780
are like prime examples of this.

01:15:24.780 --> 01:15:27.300
Or you use something called
BWT-based compressors, which

01:15:27.300 --> 01:15:30.750
we didn't talk about
in the class at all.

01:15:30.750 --> 01:15:33.318
I will let you look
at it in the homework.

01:15:33.318 --> 01:15:35.610
There is a question in the
homework that will teach you

01:15:35.610 --> 01:15:37.650
about BWT,
Burrows-Wheeler Transform,

01:15:37.650 --> 01:15:41.400
it's a way to transform the data
to make it very compressible.

01:15:41.400 --> 01:15:45.360
And BWT is faster than LZMA,
typically, at compression

01:15:45.360 --> 01:15:47.700
but slower at decompression
because the decompression

01:15:47.700 --> 01:15:48.990
is no longer just copying it.

01:15:48.990 --> 01:15:52.190
It does more advanced things.

01:15:52.190 --> 01:15:54.410
And then if you are even
more resource intensive,

01:15:54.410 --> 01:15:56.118
you should do, like,
make your own model,

01:15:56.118 --> 01:15:58.670
do context-based coding which
we learned in the last lecture.

01:15:58.670 --> 01:16:02.390
And then, if you
have nothing to do,

01:16:02.390 --> 01:16:04.430
like, you're just
sitting idle, you're

01:16:04.430 --> 01:16:08.360
OK to have your data be
compressed in 100 years,

01:16:08.360 --> 01:16:11.450
use these super [CLEARS THROAT]
small compressors.

01:16:11.450 --> 01:16:12.380
This is, today, right?

01:16:12.380 --> 01:16:14.570
Maybe five years later,
things will change.

01:16:14.570 --> 01:16:17.930
Maybe we'll have GPUs on
every machine, or something.

01:16:17.930 --> 01:16:19.050
Anyway.

01:16:19.050 --> 01:16:19.550
Right.

01:16:19.550 --> 01:16:21.710
And now, if you decide
you want to use zstandard,

01:16:21.710 --> 01:16:23.120
you want to use it correctly.

01:16:23.120 --> 01:16:27.590
Use the latest version of
this thing, or any software.

01:16:27.590 --> 01:16:29.840
Or latest stable
version, obviously.

01:16:29.840 --> 01:16:31.100
Choose the right level.

01:16:31.100 --> 01:16:32.687
There are levels 1 to 19.

01:16:32.687 --> 01:16:33.770
There are negative levels.

01:16:33.770 --> 01:16:35.600
There are some ultra
levels above 19.

01:16:35.600 --> 01:16:38.750
There is a big range
of compression speed,

01:16:38.750 --> 01:16:41.780
compression ratio trade-off
you can choose with zstandard.

01:16:41.780 --> 01:16:43.700
And sometimes you want
to go beyond this.

01:16:43.700 --> 01:16:45.890
Maybe these levels
are not enough.

01:16:45.890 --> 01:16:47.990
As we saw in the past,
you have the hash length,

01:16:47.990 --> 01:16:49.640
min-max length, dot, dot, dot.

01:16:49.640 --> 01:16:50.870
All these parameters.

01:16:50.870 --> 01:16:52.880
Levels are just like
specific combinations

01:16:52.880 --> 01:16:53.810
of these parameters.

01:16:53.810 --> 01:16:55.130
You can go even deeper.

01:16:55.130 --> 01:16:58.220
You can define your own
zstandard custom encoder

01:16:58.220 --> 01:17:02.250
by choosing this
special parameters.

01:17:02.250 --> 01:17:04.200
You can use the CLI,
you can use the library

01:17:04.200 --> 01:17:06.090
in the language of your choice.

01:17:06.090 --> 01:17:08.130
This is a common
mistake that people do.

01:17:08.130 --> 01:17:12.030
Like, if you're using zstandard
in like C or Java or Python,

01:17:12.030 --> 01:17:15.120
Rest, whatever, if you are
compressing multiple files one

01:17:15.120 --> 01:17:18.660
after the other, you don't
need to drop or delete

01:17:18.660 --> 01:17:20.130
your zstandard
context every time.

01:17:20.130 --> 01:17:21.210
You can reuse it.

01:17:21.210 --> 01:17:23.790
So that speeds up your
application quite a bit.

01:17:23.790 --> 01:17:25.770
If you're compressing
a bunch of small

01:17:25.770 --> 01:17:28.580
files one after the other.

01:17:28.580 --> 01:17:30.470
And then if you have
very, very small files,

01:17:30.470 --> 01:17:33.410
where you don't expect to find
good matches within the file,

01:17:33.410 --> 01:17:36.080
you use something called
dictionary compression, which

01:17:36.080 --> 01:17:37.940
is, again, a problem
in homework three.

01:17:37.940 --> 01:17:39.680
This is a very, very
interesting topic

01:17:39.680 --> 01:17:42.450
which I hope you will enjoy.

01:17:42.450 --> 01:17:45.480
Also, has some of my
favorite cartoon characters,

01:17:45.480 --> 01:17:48.500
so that's good.

01:17:48.500 --> 01:17:50.420
OK, and I think
this is the last.

01:17:50.420 --> 01:17:52.220
Yep.

01:17:52.220 --> 01:17:54.290
So follow up question
on [INAUDIBLE]..

01:17:54.290 --> 01:17:57.470
So the standard is
greater than gzip.

01:17:57.470 --> 01:18:00.460
Is that only true because
we have much bigger--

01:18:00.460 --> 01:18:02.420
like, in terms of
performance, we

01:18:02.420 --> 01:18:05.660
have much bigger context window,
in which we can find matches?

01:18:05.660 --> 01:18:09.045
Is it still better if we
limit it to 64 KB [INAUDIBLE]??

01:18:09.045 --> 01:18:09.920
It's a good question.

01:18:09.920 --> 01:18:11.930
He asks, is the
standard only better

01:18:11.930 --> 01:18:13.610
because we give it
a bigger window?

01:18:13.610 --> 01:18:14.190
No.

01:18:14.190 --> 01:18:15.440
There are many things they do.

01:18:15.440 --> 01:18:17.582
One thing is they replace
the Huffman coding used

01:18:17.582 --> 01:18:19.040
for the match
positions and lengths

01:18:19.040 --> 01:18:22.730
with tANS, which is a better
compressor, as we saw.

01:18:22.730 --> 01:18:24.920
It was much closer
to arithmetic coding.

01:18:24.920 --> 01:18:26.390
So that's one thing.

01:18:26.390 --> 01:18:29.300
Then zstandard has many
more advanced match finding

01:18:29.300 --> 01:18:30.170
strategies.

01:18:30.170 --> 01:18:31.940
Many of which actually
only make sense

01:18:31.940 --> 01:18:33.500
at longer context windows.

01:18:33.500 --> 01:18:37.340
So gzip uses a very simple
hash-based match finder.

01:18:37.340 --> 01:18:40.370
Zstandard has many
other things there.

01:18:40.370 --> 01:18:42.020
And the last thing
is the zstandard

01:18:42.020 --> 01:18:43.880
is just written better.

01:18:43.880 --> 01:18:46.400
Both the compressor, the
decompressor, the format,

01:18:46.400 --> 01:18:48.810
all of which is written
in a way to minimize

01:18:48.810 --> 01:18:50.100
the number of loops.

01:18:50.100 --> 01:18:52.260
Loops are very expensive
in modern hardware.

01:18:52.260 --> 01:18:55.560
So they did it in a way which is
very suitable for the today's,

01:18:55.560 --> 01:19:02.160
like, architectures, the
computer architecture stuff.

01:19:02.160 --> 01:19:04.320
So all of that
basically contributes.

01:19:04.320 --> 01:19:06.840
C-- it's not like you
shouldn't think zstandard

01:19:06.840 --> 01:19:09.210
is better compression than gzip.

01:19:09.210 --> 01:19:11.820
It's like at the same
compression ratio,

01:19:11.820 --> 01:19:16.080
zstandard is faster,
or at the same speed,

01:19:16.080 --> 01:19:18.120
zstandard gives
you a smaller file.

01:19:18.120 --> 01:19:20.820
So basically, when zstandard is
doing the same thing that gzip

01:19:20.820 --> 01:19:23.040
is doing, it does it faster.

01:19:23.040 --> 01:19:25.060
So that gives it a
boost, basically.

01:19:25.060 --> 01:19:25.560
Right?

01:19:25.560 --> 01:19:27.435
So it's not only better
compression it's also

01:19:27.435 --> 01:19:30.720
better speed because they
implemented it much better.

01:19:30.720 --> 01:19:33.240
Because the architecture's
changed over time.

01:19:33.240 --> 01:19:35.340
It's important-- we are
not saying gzip is bad.

01:19:35.340 --> 01:19:38.520
Like, when it was made, it's
a very, very efficient piece

01:19:38.520 --> 01:19:40.770
of code for the 1990s.

01:19:40.770 --> 01:19:45.630
Today, like, that's not like
the thing you want to use.

01:19:45.630 --> 01:19:49.140
So last point is
that, OK, now, you

01:19:49.140 --> 01:19:51.420
looked at all of those
you still think, no.

01:19:51.420 --> 01:19:54.390
I have exabytes
of my data, I want

01:19:54.390 --> 01:19:56.310
to make something
special for my data,

01:19:56.310 --> 01:19:58.710
or I have genomics or
some special application.

01:19:58.710 --> 01:20:00.630
I want to make my
own compressor.

01:20:00.630 --> 01:20:03.000
So just a few
things that you want

01:20:03.000 --> 01:20:05.640
to think about before you start
making your own compressor.

01:20:05.640 --> 01:20:07.710
One thing is that
you checked out

01:20:07.710 --> 01:20:10.500
zstandard and gzip and
BZIP2 and all of these,

01:20:10.500 --> 01:20:13.200
and you found that
the gap between them,

01:20:13.200 --> 01:20:15.580
and your estimate
of the entropy.

01:20:15.580 --> 01:20:17.152
You can build your own models.

01:20:17.152 --> 01:20:18.610
You make your
probability model you

01:20:18.610 --> 01:20:21.520
try to estimate what is
the entropy of my data.

01:20:21.520 --> 01:20:24.460
And you see, oh,
zstandard gives me 100 KB,

01:20:24.460 --> 01:20:26.770
but my entropy is around 50 KB.

01:20:26.770 --> 01:20:29.800
So there is like a 50 KB
gap between the entropy

01:20:29.800 --> 01:20:30.670
and my data.

01:20:30.670 --> 01:20:32.620
So therefore, maybe
I can do better.

01:20:32.620 --> 01:20:34.450
So that's sort of one thing.

01:20:34.450 --> 01:20:36.430
Another thing is that,
OK, zstandard gives me

01:20:36.430 --> 01:20:37.540
very good compression.

01:20:37.540 --> 01:20:40.090
But it just wastes a lot
of time because it doesn't

01:20:40.090 --> 01:20:42.250
know that my data is a table.

01:20:42.250 --> 01:20:45.790
Or my data is a genomic data,
or my data is a CSV file,

01:20:45.790 --> 01:20:47.920
or it's like some
specific type of data.

01:20:47.920 --> 01:20:49.930
So maybe, if I know that I'm--

01:20:49.930 --> 01:20:52.060
zstandard is a general
purpose compressor, right?

01:20:52.060 --> 01:20:54.310
So it has to work for
every type of data.

01:20:54.310 --> 01:20:57.130
But your special compressor
only needs to work for one kind

01:20:57.130 --> 01:20:59.830
of data, so it has more
flexibility in the design,

01:20:59.830 --> 01:21:02.830
it can do things that zstandard
cannot do because it knows that

01:21:02.830 --> 01:21:04.450
your data is going
to be a CSV file,

01:21:04.450 --> 01:21:10.603
or it's going to be a genomics
data set or some other thing.

01:21:10.603 --> 01:21:12.020
Another thing is,
like, if you are

01:21:12.020 --> 01:21:13.880
going to make your own
domain-specific compressor,

01:21:13.880 --> 01:21:16.490
it's easier if it's like a
closed ecosystem you're working

01:21:16.490 --> 01:21:18.020
within a company or something.

01:21:18.020 --> 01:21:21.440
Because it's not just
about the compressor.

01:21:21.440 --> 01:21:24.050
If there are 100
different people reading

01:21:24.050 --> 01:21:28.370
the compressed files with their
own libraries, their own tools,

01:21:28.370 --> 01:21:29.810
you suddenly break everyone.

01:21:29.810 --> 01:21:32.180
As soon as you say, oh, I'm
not using gzip or zstandard,

01:21:32.180 --> 01:21:36.290
I will use my own, like,
[INAUDIBLE] or something

01:21:36.290 --> 01:21:36.880
like that.

01:21:36.880 --> 01:21:38.630
I will make my own
compressor, I will just

01:21:38.630 --> 01:21:39.887
use my own compressor.

01:21:39.887 --> 01:21:42.470
But the other people don't know
how to decode your compressor.

01:21:42.470 --> 01:21:43.550
So how will that work?

01:21:43.550 --> 01:21:47.150
So your thing should only-- will
only work if it's a closed--

01:21:47.150 --> 01:21:50.210
it's much easier to work
if it's a closed ecosystem.

01:21:50.210 --> 01:21:53.480
And final thing is,
like, it's only worth

01:21:53.480 --> 01:21:55.580
it if you have a lot
of data of that kind,

01:21:55.580 --> 01:21:57.673
or you can't live
without it, basically.

01:21:57.673 --> 01:21:59.090
You should have a
very strong case

01:21:59.090 --> 01:22:01.790
because a compressor
needs design.

01:22:01.790 --> 01:22:02.900
It needs implementation.

01:22:02.900 --> 01:22:04.790
It needs a lot of testing.

01:22:04.790 --> 01:22:07.070
You need to create a
compression format, right?

01:22:07.070 --> 01:22:11.060
You did that float question when
you did the assignment, right?

01:22:11.060 --> 01:22:14.210
It was so tricky to
store those floats.

01:22:14.210 --> 01:22:17.480
So many different ways of doing
that with all their trade-offs.

01:22:17.480 --> 01:22:19.343
Think about a
full-fledged compressor.

01:22:19.343 --> 01:22:21.510
So many different choices
you need to make when you,

01:22:21.510 --> 01:22:23.100
like, define the format.

01:22:23.100 --> 01:22:24.990
And once you define the
format, and you say,

01:22:24.990 --> 01:22:27.870
I'm at version one, then
you're stuck basically.

01:22:27.870 --> 01:22:30.420
Then you should not
change your compressor.

01:22:30.420 --> 01:22:33.120
If you change your format, then
your-- other people's decoders

01:22:33.120 --> 01:22:33.780
will break.

01:22:33.780 --> 01:22:35.790
And everybody will
be very unhappy.

01:22:35.790 --> 01:22:38.040
I got a zstandard file,
but your zstandard decoder

01:22:38.040 --> 01:22:38.970
is not working.

01:22:38.970 --> 01:22:41.340
So just you need
to be very careful

01:22:41.340 --> 01:22:44.070
about backwards compatibility,
that sort of thing.

01:22:44.070 --> 01:22:46.110
You need to monitor
how it's working.

01:22:46.110 --> 01:22:47.340
You will find bugs, right?

01:22:47.340 --> 01:22:49.110
Gzip, even if it's,
like, 30 years old

01:22:49.110 --> 01:22:50.970
they found a bug in
it like last year.

01:22:50.970 --> 01:22:52.500
So it's still, like--

01:22:52.500 --> 01:22:55.290
you need to maintain it
for a very, very long time.

01:22:55.290 --> 01:22:58.080
And then you need to not
only implement it once

01:22:58.080 --> 01:22:59.610
in C or Python,
or something, you

01:22:59.610 --> 01:23:01.632
need to have it be
supported in, like--

01:23:01.632 --> 01:23:03.090
you should have a
Java library, you

01:23:03.090 --> 01:23:05.790
should have a C library,
Python library, dot, dot, dot.

01:23:05.790 --> 01:23:08.520
You need to have it work
on today's machines.

01:23:08.520 --> 01:23:11.040
You need to have it work on,
like, yesterday's machines.

01:23:11.040 --> 01:23:13.110
And, like, 30-year-old
processors,

01:23:13.110 --> 01:23:15.790
different architectures,
different operating systems.

01:23:15.790 --> 01:23:17.430
So yeah.

01:23:17.430 --> 01:23:19.200
Only do it if it's
worth it, basically.

01:23:19.200 --> 01:23:21.870
That is the point.

01:23:21.870 --> 01:23:22.470
Like, yeah.

01:23:22.470 --> 01:23:24.360
In a big company, you
can often find cases

01:23:24.360 --> 01:23:27.390
where it is worth it, or if
that is your whole product,

01:23:27.390 --> 01:23:29.290
maybe you find a case
where it is worth it.

01:23:29.290 --> 01:23:29.790
Right?

01:23:29.790 --> 01:23:31.790
Genomics, for example,
is a domain where there's

01:23:31.790 --> 01:23:33.120
a lot of data of the same kind.

01:23:33.120 --> 01:23:35.730
And it is definitely worth
it doing a custom compressor,

01:23:35.730 --> 01:23:37.110
and they did it basically.

01:23:37.110 --> 01:23:40.320
But then everybody writes a
paper improving it slightly

01:23:40.320 --> 01:23:41.490
in a breaking manner.

01:23:41.490 --> 01:23:45.343
But nobody uses those
particular things, right?

01:23:45.343 --> 01:23:47.760
Basically, if you have a million
different domain-specific

01:23:47.760 --> 01:23:51.660
compressors, how is anybody
going to inter-operate?

01:23:51.660 --> 01:23:54.840
Like we have our language like
English or any other language.

01:23:54.840 --> 01:23:56.888
If everybody decided, I
will use my own language,

01:23:56.888 --> 01:23:57.930
that's not going to work.

01:23:57.930 --> 01:23:59.190
So just-- yeah.

01:23:59.190 --> 01:24:02.040
There is a limit to when
domain-specific compressors

01:24:02.040 --> 01:24:02.610
work.

01:24:02.610 --> 01:24:03.780
But they do work.

01:24:03.780 --> 01:24:08.410
And if you have a domain
that you are interested in,

01:24:08.410 --> 01:24:10.450
and if you think a
domain-specific compressor is

01:24:10.450 --> 01:24:13.300
the right thing, please
come to us and we'll

01:24:13.300 --> 01:24:14.300
do a project, basically.

01:24:14.300 --> 01:24:14.800
Yeah.

01:24:14.800 --> 01:24:16.180
Do a project on that, basically.

01:24:16.180 --> 01:24:19.410
Last year, like, people
did on MRI data, I think,

01:24:19.410 --> 01:24:21.520
and a few other types
of specific data

01:24:21.520 --> 01:24:24.520
that they were working with.

01:24:24.520 --> 01:24:26.720
So that's all on
lossless compression.

01:24:26.720 --> 01:24:29.530
[INAUDIBLE] will see
you on Wednesday.

01:24:29.530 --> 01:24:30.340
Thank you.

01:24:30.340 --> 01:24:32.430
Thank you for your patience.

